1
00:00:04,170 --> 00:00:12,512
Ripped with SubRip 1.17 and Verified by CdinT
cdint@hotmail.com

2
00:00:12,554 --> 00:00:17,517
I deliver perfection...
and don't brag about it! :D

3
00:02:43,121 --> 00:02:45,874
{y:i}The hills are alive

4
00:02:46,124 --> 00:02:50,545
{y:i}With the sound of music

5
00:02:50,795 --> 00:02:53,256
{y:i}With songs they have sung

6
00:02:53,506 --> 00:02:58,386
{y:i}For a thousand years

7
00:02:58,636 --> 00:03:01,765
{y:i}The hills fill my heart

8
00:03:02,015 --> 00:03:06,603
{y:i}With the sound of music

9
00:03:06,853 --> 00:03:14,277
{y:i}My heart wants to sing
{y:i}Every song it hears

10
00:03:14,527 --> 00:03:19,366
{y:i}My heart wants to beat like the wings
{y:i}Of the birds that rise

11
00:03:19,616 --> 00:03:22,702
{y:i}From the lake to the trees

12
00:03:22,952 --> 00:03:27,248
{y:i}My heart wants to sigh
{y:i}Like a chime that flies

13
00:03:27,499 --> 00:03:31,461
{y:i}From a church on a breeze

14
00:03:31,711 --> 00:03:35,215
{y:i}To laugh like a brook
{y:i}When it trips and falls

15
00:03:35,465 --> 00:03:38,968
{y:i}Over stones on its way

16
00:03:39,219 --> 00:03:43,098
{y:i}To sing through the night

17
00:03:43,348 --> 00:03:47,227
{y:i}Like a lark who is learning to pray

18
00:03:47,477 --> 00:03:53,066
{y:i}I go to the hills

19
00:03:53,316 --> 00:03:59,489
{y:i}When my heart is lonely

20
00:03:59,739 --> 00:04:03,576
{y:i}I know I will hear

21
00:04:03,827 --> 00:04:08,331
{y:i}What I've heard before

22
00:04:08,581 --> 00:04:13,294
{y:i}My heart will be blessed

23
00:04:13,545 --> 00:04:22,137
{y:i}With the sound of music

24
00:04:22,387 --> 00:04:27,517
{y:i}And I'll sing...

25
00:04:27,767 --> 00:04:38,278
{y:i}... once more

26
00:09:18,808 --> 00:09:23,772
{y:i}Hallelujah, hallelujah

27
00:09:24,022 --> 00:09:30,195
{y:i}Hallelujah, hallelujah

28
00:09:31,029 --> 00:09:33,031
- Reverend Mother.
- Sister Bernice.

29
00:09:33,281 --> 00:09:36,743
- I simply cannot find her.
- Maria?

30
00:09:36,993 --> 00:09:38,912
She's missing again.

31
00:09:39,162 --> 00:09:41,581
We should've put a cowbell
around her neck.

32
00:09:41,831 --> 00:09:44,876
Have you tried the barn? You know
how much she adores the animals.

33
00:09:45,126 --> 00:09:47,837
I have looked everywhere.
In all of the usual places.

34
00:09:48,088 --> 00:09:50,340
Sister, considering it's Maria...

35
00:09:50,590 --> 00:09:54,761
...I suggest you look in someplace
unusual.

36
00:09:58,515 --> 00:09:59,766
Well, Reverend Mother...

37
00:10:00,016 --> 00:10:02,644
...I hope this new infraction ends
whatever doubts...

38
00:10:02,894 --> 00:10:06,106
...you may still have
about Maria's future here.

39
00:10:06,356 --> 00:10:10,402
I always try to keep faith
in my doubts, Sister Berthe.

40
00:10:10,652 --> 00:10:13,530
After all, the wool of a black sheep
is just as warm.

41
00:10:13,780 --> 00:10:18,076
We are not talking about sheep,
black or white, Sister Margaretta.

42
00:10:18,326 --> 00:10:21,746
Of all the candidates for
the novitiate, Maria is the least...

43
00:10:21,996 --> 00:10:24,749
Children, children.

44
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:29,337
We were speculating about
the qualifications of our postulants.

45
00:10:29,587 --> 00:10:32,590
The Mistress of Novices
and the Mistress of Postulants...

46
00:10:32,841 --> 00:10:38,138
...were trying to help me
by expressing opposite points of view.

47
00:10:38,388 --> 00:10:41,391
Tell me, Sister Catherine,
what do you think of Maria?

48
00:10:41,641 --> 00:10:45,437
She's a wonderful girl,
some of the time.

49
00:10:45,687 --> 00:10:48,773
- Sister Agatha?
- It's very easy to like Maria...

50
00:10:49,024 --> 00:10:51,568
...except when it's difficult.

51
00:10:51,818 --> 00:10:55,363
- And you, Sister Sophia?
- Oh, I love her very dearly.

52
00:10:55,613 --> 00:10:58,533
But she always seems to be
in trouble, doesn't she?

53
00:10:58,783 --> 00:11:00,618
Exactly what I say.

54
00:11:00,869 --> 00:11:03,288
{y:i}She climbs a tree and scrapes her knee

55
00:11:03,538 --> 00:11:05,623
{y:i}Her dress has got a tear

56
00:11:05,874 --> 00:11:10,462
{y:i}She waltzes on her way to Mass
{y:i}And whistles on the stair

57
00:11:10,712 --> 00:11:15,216
{y:i}And underneath her wimple
{y:i}She has curlers in her hair

58
00:11:15,467 --> 00:11:20,055
{y:i}I've even heard her singing
{y:i}In the abbey

59
00:11:20,305 --> 00:11:22,265
{y:i}She's always late for chapel

60
00:11:22,515 --> 00:11:24,851
{y:i}But her penitence is real

61
00:11:25,101 --> 00:11:27,395
{y:i}She's always late for everything

62
00:11:27,645 --> 00:11:29,814
{y:i}Except for every meal

63
00:11:30,065 --> 00:11:34,736
{y:i}I hate to have to say it
{y:i}But I very firmly feel

64
00:11:34,986 --> 00:11:39,657
{y:i}Maria's not an asset to the abbey

65
00:11:39,908 --> 00:11:42,452
{y:i}I'd like to say a word in her behalf

66
00:11:42,702 --> 00:11:44,662
Say it, Sister Margaretta.

67
00:11:44,913 --> 00:11:50,418
{y:i}Maria makes me laugh

68
00:11:51,795 --> 00:11:55,674
{y:i}How do you solve a problem like Maria?

69
00:11:55,924 --> 00:11:59,761
{y:i}How do you catch a cloud
{y:i}And pin it down?

70
00:12:00,011 --> 00:12:03,640
{y:i}How do you find a word
{y:i}That means Maria?

71
00:12:03,890 --> 00:12:04,974
{y:i}A flibbertigibbet

72
00:12:05,225 --> 00:12:08,144
{y:i}- A will-o'-the-wisp
{y:i}- A clown

73
00:12:08,228 --> 00:12:11,981
{y:i}Many a thing you know
{y:i}You'd like to tell her

74
00:12:12,232 --> 00:12:15,860
{y:i}Many a thing she ought to understand

75
00:12:16,111 --> 00:12:20,115
{y:i}But how do you make her stay
{y:i}And listen to all you say?

76
00:12:20,365 --> 00:12:23,868
{y:i}How do you keep a wave upon the sand?

77
00:12:24,119 --> 00:12:28,456
{y:i}How do you solve a problem like Maria?

78
00:12:28,707 --> 00:12:34,212
{y:i}How do you hold a moonbeam...

79
00:12:34,462 --> 00:12:40,760
{y:i}... in your hand?

80
00:12:41,011 --> 00:12:44,514
{y:i}When I'm with her I'm confused
{y:i}Out of focus and bemused

81
00:12:44,764 --> 00:12:48,184
{y:i}And I never know exactly where I am

82
00:12:48,435 --> 00:12:51,855
{y:i}- Unpredictable as weather
{y:i}- She's as flighty as a feather

83
00:12:52,105 --> 00:12:53,898
{y:i}- She's a darling
{y:i}- She's a demon

84
00:12:54,149 --> 00:12:55,650
{y:i}She's a lamb

85
00:12:55,900 --> 00:12:59,529
{y:i}She'll out pester any pest
{y:i}Drive a hornet from its nest

86
00:12:59,779 --> 00:13:03,366
{y:i}She can throw a whirling dervish
{y:i}Out of whirl

87
00:13:03,616 --> 00:13:07,495
{y:i}- She is gentle, she is wild
{y:i}- She's a riddle, she's a child

88
00:13:07,746 --> 00:13:10,290
{y:i}- She's a headache
{y:i}- She's an angel

89
00:13:10,540 --> 00:13:14,961
{y:i}She's a girl

90
00:13:15,211 --> 00:13:19,049
{y:i}How do you solve a problem like Maria?

91
00:13:19,299 --> 00:13:23,053
{y:i}How do you catch a cloud
{y:i}And pin it down?

92
00:13:23,303 --> 00:13:26,848
{y:i}How do you find a word
{y:i}That means Maria?

93
00:13:27,098 --> 00:13:29,059
{y:i}- A flibbertigibbet
{y:i}- A will-o'-the-wisp

94
00:13:29,309 --> 00:13:31,186
{y:i}A clown

95
00:13:31,436 --> 00:13:35,190
{y:i}Many a thing you know
{y:i}You'd like to tell her

96
00:13:35,440 --> 00:13:38,860
{y:i}Many a thing she ought to understand

97
00:13:39,110 --> 00:13:43,490
{y:i}- But how do you make her stay
{y:i}- And listen to all you say?

98
00:13:43,740 --> 00:13:47,786
{y:i}How do you keep a wave upon the sand?

99
00:13:48,036 --> 00:13:53,833
{y:i}How do you solve a problem like Maria?

100
00:14:09,808 --> 00:14:17,190
{y:i}How do you hold a moonbeam...

101
00:14:17,440 --> 00:14:25,448
{y:i}... in your hand?

102
00:14:35,709 --> 00:14:39,254
You may go in now, Maria.

103
00:14:50,306 --> 00:14:53,476
Come here, my child.

104
00:14:58,440 --> 00:15:00,483
Now sit down.

105
00:15:00,734 --> 00:15:04,320
Reverend Mother, I'm sorry.
I couldn't help myself.

106
00:15:04,571 --> 00:15:07,699
- The hills were beckoning and before...
- Dear.

107
00:15:07,949 --> 00:15:09,909
I haven't summoned you for apologies.

108
00:15:10,160 --> 00:15:12,787
Please let me ask for forgiveness.

109
00:15:13,038 --> 00:15:14,956
If you'll feel better.

110
00:15:15,206 --> 00:15:17,584
Yes, you see,
the sky was so blue today...

111
00:15:17,834 --> 00:15:21,629
...and everything was so green and
fragrant, I had to be a part of it.

112
00:15:21,880 --> 00:15:26,301
The Untersberg led me higher like
it wanted me to go through the clouds.

113
00:15:26,551 --> 00:15:28,636
Suppose darkness had come
and you were lost?

114
00:15:28,887 --> 00:15:31,139
Mother, I could never be lost
up there.

115
00:15:31,389 --> 00:15:34,184
That's my mountain.
I was brought up on it.

116
00:15:34,434 --> 00:15:36,144
It was the mountain that led me
to you.

117
00:15:36,394 --> 00:15:37,270
Oh?

118
00:15:37,520 --> 00:15:40,482
When I was a child,
I would come down and climb a tree...

119
00:15:40,732 --> 00:15:42,484
...and look in your garden.

120
00:15:42,734 --> 00:15:46,780
I'd see the sisters at work
and hear them sing.

121
00:15:47,030 --> 00:15:50,617
Which brings me to another
transgression, Reverend Mother.

122
00:15:50,867 --> 00:15:53,411
I was singing out there today.

123
00:15:53,661 --> 00:15:57,665
Only in the abbey do we have rules
about postulants singing.

124
00:15:57,916 --> 00:15:59,918
I can't stop wherever I am.

125
00:16:00,168 --> 00:16:02,504
Worse, I can't seem to stop
saying things.

126
00:16:02,754 --> 00:16:05,006
Everything I think and feel.

127
00:16:05,256 --> 00:16:06,675
Some call that "honesty".

128
00:16:06,925 --> 00:16:09,302
Oh, but it's terrible,
Reverend Mother!

129
00:16:09,552 --> 00:16:13,556
You know how Sister Berthe makes me
kiss the floor after a disagreement?

130
00:16:13,807 --> 00:16:18,853
Lately, I kiss the floor
when I see her coming to save time.

131
00:16:19,979 --> 00:16:21,898
Maria...

132
00:16:22,148 --> 00:16:25,151
...when you saw us over the wall
and longed to be with us...

133
00:16:25,402 --> 00:16:31,199
...that didn't mean you were prepared
for the way we live here, did it?

134
00:16:31,449 --> 00:16:33,827
No, Mother, but I pray and I try.

135
00:16:34,077 --> 00:16:35,787
And I am learning. I really am.

136
00:16:36,037 --> 00:16:39,124
What is the most important lesson
you have learned here?

137
00:16:39,374 --> 00:16:44,713
To find out what is the will of God
and do it wholeheartedly.

138
00:16:46,006 --> 00:16:48,383
Maria...

139
00:16:48,633 --> 00:16:51,428
...it seems to be God's will
that you leave us.

140
00:16:51,678 --> 00:16:53,430
- Leave?
- Only for a while.

141
00:16:53,680 --> 00:16:56,016
No, Mother!
Please don't send me away!

142
00:16:56,266 --> 00:17:00,937
This is where I belong. It's my home,
my family. It's my life.

143
00:17:01,187 --> 00:17:04,149
- Are you truly ready for it?
- Yes, I am.

144
00:17:04,399 --> 00:17:09,320
If you go out into the world for a
time, knowing what we expect of you...

145
00:17:09,571 --> 00:17:12,282
...you will find out
if you can expect it of yourself.

146
00:17:12,532 --> 00:17:18,371
I know what you expect, Mother,
and I can do it! I promise I can!

147
00:17:20,373 --> 00:17:23,168
Yes, Mother.

148
00:17:23,710 --> 00:17:26,921
If it is God's will.

149
00:17:27,630 --> 00:17:32,719
There is a family near Salzburg that
needs a governess until September.

150
00:17:32,969 --> 00:17:35,555
- September?
- For seven children.

151
00:17:35,805 --> 00:17:36,848
Seven children?!

152
00:17:37,098 --> 00:17:38,892
Do you like children?

153
00:17:39,142 --> 00:17:41,853
Well, yes, but seven!

154
00:17:42,103 --> 00:17:45,565
I will tell Captain von Trapp
to expect you tomorrow.

155
00:17:45,815 --> 00:17:47,650
Captain?

156
00:17:47,901 --> 00:17:52,739
A retired officer of the lmperial
Navy. A fine man and a brave one.

157
00:17:52,989 --> 00:17:55,658
His wife died,
and he is alone with the children.

158
00:17:55,909 --> 00:18:00,747
I understand he has had a difficult
time keeping a governess there.

159
00:18:00,997 --> 00:18:04,417
Why difficult, Reverend Mother?

160
00:18:04,668 --> 00:18:09,130
The Lord will show you
in His own good time.

161
00:18:44,416 --> 00:18:46,876
When the Lord closes a door...

162
00:18:47,127 --> 00:18:51,172
...somewhere He opens a window.

163
00:18:52,007 --> 00:18:56,970
{y:i}What will this day be like?

164
00:18:57,220 --> 00:19:00,974
{y:i}I wonder

165
00:19:05,937 --> 00:19:10,650
{y:i}What will my future be?

166
00:19:10,900 --> 00:19:14,696
{y:i}I wonder

167
00:19:14,738 --> 00:19:17,741
{y:i}It could be so exciting

168
00:19:17,991 --> 00:19:21,077
{y:i}To be out in the world
{y:i}To be free

169
00:19:21,327 --> 00:19:25,331
{y:i}My heart should be wildly rejoicing

170
00:19:25,582 --> 00:19:31,004
{y:i}Oh, what's the matter with me?

171
00:19:31,254 --> 00:19:34,299
{y:i}I've always longed for adventure

172
00:19:34,549 --> 00:19:38,136
{y:i}To do the things I've never dared

173
00:19:38,386 --> 00:19:41,598
{y:i}Now here I'm facing adventure

174
00:19:41,848 --> 00:19:49,522
{y:i}Then why am I so scared?

175
00:19:51,441 --> 00:19:54,319
{y:i}A captain with seven children

176
00:19:54,569 --> 00:19:58,948
{y:i}What's so fearsome about that?

177
00:19:59,949 --> 00:20:02,535
{y:i}I must stop these doubts and worries

178
00:20:02,786 --> 00:20:05,663
{y:i}If I don't I just know I'll turn back

179
00:20:05,914 --> 00:20:09,876
{y:i}I must dream of the things
{y:i}I am seeking

180
00:20:10,126 --> 00:20:15,840
{y:i}I am seeking the courage I lack

181
00:20:16,091 --> 00:20:19,928
{y:i}The courage to serve them
{y:i}With reliance

182
00:20:20,178 --> 00:20:23,098
{y:i}Face my mistakes without defiance

183
00:20:23,348 --> 00:20:25,058
{y:i}Show them I'm worthy

184
00:20:25,308 --> 00:20:27,977
{y:i}And while I show them

185
00:20:28,228 --> 00:20:31,981
{y:i}I'll show me

186
00:20:32,232 --> 00:20:37,612
{y:i}So let them bring on
{y:i}All their problems

187
00:20:37,862 --> 00:20:41,116
{y:i}I'll do better than my best

188
00:20:41,366 --> 00:20:44,452
{y:i}I have confidence
{y:i}They'll put me to the test

189
00:20:44,703 --> 00:20:48,123
{y:i}But I'll make them see
{y:i}I have confidence in me

190
00:20:48,373 --> 00:20:51,668
{y:i}Somehow I will impress them

191
00:20:51,918 --> 00:20:55,171
{y:i}I will be firm but kind

192
00:20:55,422 --> 00:20:58,717
{y:i}And all those children
{y:i}Heaven bless them

193
00:20:58,967 --> 00:21:02,262
{y:i}They will look up to me
{y:i}And mind me

194
00:21:02,512 --> 00:21:05,640
{y:i}With each step I am more certain

195
00:21:05,890 --> 00:21:09,102
{y:i}Everything will turn out fine

196
00:21:09,352 --> 00:21:12,439
{y:i}I have confidence
{y:i}The world can all be mine

197
00:21:12,689 --> 00:21:19,738
{y:i}They'll have to agree
{y:i}I have confidence in me

198
00:21:19,738 --> 00:21:22,490
{y:i}I have confidence in sunshine

199
00:21:22,741 --> 00:21:25,785
{y:i}I have confidence in rain

200
00:21:26,036 --> 00:21:28,955
{y:i}I have confidence
{y:i}That spring will come again

201
00:21:29,205 --> 00:21:32,292
{y:i}Besides which you see
{y:i}I have confidence in me

202
00:21:32,542 --> 00:21:35,670
{y:i}Strength doesn't lie in numbers

203
00:21:35,920 --> 00:21:38,965
{y:i}Strength doesn't lie in wealth

204
00:21:39,215 --> 00:21:42,510
{y:i}Strength lies in nights
{y:i}Of peaceful slumbers

205
00:21:42,761 --> 00:21:45,680
{y:i}When you wake up, wake up
{y:i}It's healthy

206
00:21:45,930 --> 00:21:48,933
{y:i}All I trust I leave my heart to

207
00:21:49,184 --> 00:21:52,062
{y:i}All I trust becomes my own

208
00:21:52,312 --> 00:21:58,651
{y:i}I have confidence in confidence alone

209
00:22:11,748 --> 00:22:14,918
Oh, help.

210
00:22:25,553 --> 00:22:32,727
{y:i}I have confidence in confidence alone

211
00:22:32,977 --> 00:22:37,816
{y:i}Besides which you see
{y:i}I have confidence...

212
00:22:38,066 --> 00:22:45,323
{y:i}... in me

213
00:22:47,867 --> 00:22:51,037
Oh. Whoo.

214
00:22:51,454 --> 00:22:55,375
Hello. Here I am.

215
00:22:56,626 --> 00:23:00,046
I'm from the convent.
I'm the new governess, captain.

216
00:23:00,171 --> 00:23:03,633
And I'm the old butler, fräulein.

217
00:23:03,883 --> 00:23:05,093
Oh.

218
00:23:05,343 --> 00:23:08,722
Well, how do you do?

219
00:23:17,689 --> 00:23:23,319
You'll wait here, please.

220
00:24:48,113 --> 00:24:53,118
In future, remember certain rooms
in this house are not to be disturbed.

221
00:24:53,368 --> 00:24:56,413
Yes, captain, sir.

222
00:25:02,752 --> 00:25:09,009
- Why do you stare at me that way?
- You don't look like a sea captain.

223
00:25:09,259 --> 00:25:12,762
I'm afraid you don't look
very much like a governess.

224
00:25:13,013 --> 00:25:14,180
- Turn around.
- What?

225
00:25:14,431 --> 00:25:17,183
Turn.

226
00:25:18,852 --> 00:25:21,563
Hat off.

227
00:25:23,231 --> 00:25:25,859
Put on another dress
before meeting the children.

228
00:25:26,109 --> 00:25:27,736
But I don't have another.

229
00:25:27,986 --> 00:25:30,864
When we enter the abbey,
our worldly clothes go to the poor.

230
00:25:31,114 --> 00:25:32,323
What about this one?

231
00:25:32,574 --> 00:25:34,200
The poor didn't want it.

232
00:25:34,451 --> 00:25:35,493
Hm.

233
00:25:35,744 --> 00:25:40,248
There wasn't time to make a new dress.
I can make clothes.

234
00:25:40,498 --> 00:25:43,209
I'll see that you get some material.

235
00:25:43,460 --> 00:25:45,795
Today, if possible.

236
00:25:46,046 --> 00:25:47,797
- Now, fräulein...
- Maria.

237
00:25:48,048 --> 00:25:50,717
- I don't know how much they told you.
- Not much.

238
00:25:50,967 --> 00:25:53,553
You are the twelfth governess...

239
00:25:53,803 --> 00:25:56,222
...to look after my children
since their mother died.

240
00:25:56,473 --> 00:25:58,683
I trust you will be an improvement
on the last one.

241
00:25:58,933 --> 00:26:02,312
She stayed only two hours.

242
00:26:02,479 --> 00:26:06,274
What's wrong with the children, sir?

243
00:26:06,524 --> 00:26:10,945
Nothing is wrong with the children,
only the governesses.

244
00:26:11,196 --> 00:26:16,785
They could not maintain discipline,
without which the house cannot be run.

245
00:26:17,035 --> 00:26:18,870
Drill them in their studies.

246
00:26:19,120 --> 00:26:22,624
I will not permit them to dream
away their summer holidays.

247
00:26:22,874 --> 00:26:25,543
Each afternoon,
they march, breathing deeply.

248
00:26:25,794 --> 00:26:27,420
Bedtime is to be strictly observed.

249
00:26:27,671 --> 00:26:28,588
When do they play?

250
00:26:28,838 --> 00:26:32,884
You will see to it that they conduct
themselves with the utmost decorum.

251
00:26:33,134 --> 00:26:37,639
- I am placing you in command.
- Yes, sir.

252
00:27:56,718 --> 00:27:58,219
Now...

253
00:27:58,470 --> 00:28:02,974
...this is your new governess,
Fräulein Maria.

254
00:28:03,099 --> 00:28:05,143
Give your name at your signal.

255
00:28:05,393 --> 00:28:09,230
Fräulein, listen carefully. Learn
their signals so you can call them.

256
00:28:10,857 --> 00:28:12,275
Liesl.

257
00:28:12,525 --> 00:28:14,277
Friedrich.

258
00:28:14,527 --> 00:28:16,071
Louisa.

259
00:28:16,321 --> 00:28:17,572
Kurt.

260
00:28:17,822 --> 00:28:18,865
Brigitta.

261
00:28:19,115 --> 00:28:20,408
Marta.

262
00:28:26,790 --> 00:28:29,668
Gretl.

263
00:28:32,754 --> 00:28:36,174
Now, let's see how well you listened.

264
00:28:36,424 --> 00:28:38,802
I won't need to whistle for them,
Reverend Captain.

265
00:28:39,052 --> 00:28:42,472
I mean, I'll use their names.
Such lovely names.

266
00:28:42,722 --> 00:28:45,892
Fräulein, this is a large house.
The grounds are extensive.

267
00:28:46,142 --> 00:28:48,895
And I will not have anyone shouting.

268
00:28:49,145 --> 00:28:50,814
You will take this, please.
Learn to use it.

269
00:28:51,064 --> 00:28:54,275
The children will help you.

270
00:28:54,776 --> 00:28:59,364
Now, when I want you,
this is what you will hear.

271
00:28:59,614 --> 00:29:02,075
Oh, no, sir. I'm sorry, sir!

272
00:29:02,325 --> 00:29:03,827
I could never answer to a whistle.

273
00:29:04,077 --> 00:29:06,705
Whistles are for animals,
not for children.

274
00:29:06,955 --> 00:29:08,998
And definitely not for me.

275
00:29:09,249 --> 00:29:12,460
It would be too humiliating.

276
00:29:12,711 --> 00:29:17,966
Fräulein, were you this much trouble
at the abbey?

277
00:29:18,216 --> 00:29:20,885
Oh, much more, sir.

278
00:29:30,437 --> 00:29:33,231
I don't know your signal.

279
00:29:33,481 --> 00:29:37,652
You may call me "captain".

280
00:29:49,039 --> 00:29:52,042
At ease.

281
00:29:53,293 --> 00:29:54,836
Now that there's just us...

282
00:29:55,086 --> 00:29:59,507
...would you please tell me all your
names again and how old you are.

283
00:29:59,758 --> 00:30:04,262
I'm Liesl. I'm 16 years old,
and I don't need a governess.

284
00:30:04,512 --> 00:30:09,142
I'm glad you told me, Liesl.
We'll just be good friends.

285
00:30:09,726 --> 00:30:13,563
I'm Friedrich. I'm 14.
I'm impossible.

286
00:30:13,813 --> 00:30:15,482
Really? Who told you that, Friedrich?

287
00:30:15,732 --> 00:30:19,444
Fräulein Josephine.
Four governesses ago.

288
00:30:19,694 --> 00:30:22,697
I'm Brigitta.

289
00:30:22,989 --> 00:30:27,452
You didn't tell me how old you are,
Louisa.

290
00:30:27,702 --> 00:30:29,954
I'm Brigitta. She's Louisa.

291
00:30:30,205 --> 00:30:32,957
She's 13 years old, and you're smart.

292
00:30:33,208 --> 00:30:37,045
I'm 10, and I think your dress
is the ugliest one I ever saw.

293
00:30:37,295 --> 00:30:39,881
- Brigitta, you shouldn't say that.
- Why not?

294
00:30:40,131 --> 00:30:42,425
- Don't you think it's ugly?
- Of course.

295
00:30:42,676 --> 00:30:45,387
But Fräulein Helga's was ugliest.

296
00:30:45,637 --> 00:30:48,264
I'm Kurt. I'm 11.
I'm incorrigible.

297
00:30:48,515 --> 00:30:51,643
- Congratulations.
- What's "incorrigible"?

298
00:30:51,893 --> 00:30:57,315
I think it means you want
to be treated like a boy.

299
00:30:57,941 --> 00:31:01,236
I'm Marta, and I'm going to be
seven on Tuesday.

300
00:31:01,486 --> 00:31:03,822
I'd like a pink parasol.

301
00:31:04,072 --> 00:31:07,158
Pink's my favorite color too.

302
00:31:07,951 --> 00:31:09,786
Yes, you're Gretl.

303
00:31:10,036 --> 00:31:11,955
And you're five years old?

304
00:31:12,205 --> 00:31:15,750
My, you're practically a lady.

305
00:31:16,710 --> 00:31:20,922
I have to tell you a secret.
I've never been a governess.

306
00:31:21,172 --> 00:31:23,967
You don't know anything
about being a governess?

307
00:31:24,217 --> 00:31:26,261
Nothing. I'll need lots of advice.

308
00:31:26,511 --> 00:31:31,057
The best way to start is to tell
Father to mind his own business.

309
00:31:31,307 --> 00:31:33,059
Never come to dinner on time.

310
00:31:33,309 --> 00:31:36,062
Never eat your soup quietly.

311
00:31:36,312 --> 00:31:38,356
During dessert, always blow your nose.

312
00:31:38,606 --> 00:31:41,359
Don't you believe a word they say,
Fräulein Maria.

313
00:31:41,609 --> 00:31:45,530
- Oh, why not?
- Because I like you.

314
00:31:45,780 --> 00:31:47,741
Children, outside for your walk.

315
00:31:47,991 --> 00:31:49,826
Father's orders. Hurry up.

316
00:31:50,076 --> 00:31:51,369
Quick, quick, quick.

317
00:31:51,619 --> 00:31:54,831
Fräulein Maria, I'm Frau Schmidt,
the housekeeper.

318
00:31:55,081 --> 00:31:56,082
How do you do.

319
00:31:56,332 --> 00:32:00,712
I'll show you to your room.
Follow me.

320
00:32:10,305 --> 00:32:13,600
Poor little dears.

321
00:32:24,778 --> 00:32:30,658
You're very lucky. With
Fräulein Helga it was a snake.

322
00:32:43,046 --> 00:32:45,507
Good evening.

323
00:32:45,757 --> 00:32:49,302
- Good evening, children.
- Good evening, Fräulein Maria.

324
00:32:56,518 --> 00:32:58,144
Enchanting little ritual.

325
00:32:58,395 --> 00:33:02,065
Something you learned at the abbey?

326
00:33:02,315 --> 00:33:06,152
No.

327
00:33:06,277 --> 00:33:09,114
Rheumatism.

328
00:33:17,330 --> 00:33:22,043
Excuse me, captain. Haven't we
forgotten to thank the Lord?

329
00:33:28,717 --> 00:33:32,012
For what we receive,
may the Lord make us truly thankful.

330
00:33:32,262 --> 00:33:35,724
- Amen.
Amen.

331
00:33:38,727 --> 00:33:40,603
I'd like to thank you all...

332
00:33:40,854 --> 00:33:45,400
...for the precious gift
you left in my pocket today.

333
00:33:47,318 --> 00:33:50,697
What gift?

334
00:33:52,282 --> 00:33:54,409
It's a secret between
the children and me.

335
00:33:54,659 --> 00:33:58,455
Then I suggest you keep it,
and let us eat.

336
00:33:58,705 --> 00:34:00,665
Knowing how nervous
I must have been...

337
00:34:00,915 --> 00:34:03,293
...a stranger in a new household...

338
00:34:03,543 --> 00:34:06,629
...knowing how important it was
for me to feel accepted...

339
00:34:06,880 --> 00:34:11,051
...it was so kind and thoughtful of
you to make my first moments here...

340
00:34:11,301 --> 00:34:13,511
...so warm and happy...

341
00:34:13,762 --> 00:34:17,057
...and pleasant.

342
00:34:24,773 --> 00:34:28,360
- What is the matter, Marta?
- Nothing.

343
00:34:39,204 --> 00:34:40,246
Fräulein...

344
00:34:40,497 --> 00:34:44,292
...is it to be at every meal
or merely at dinnertime...

345
00:34:44,542 --> 00:34:49,089
...that you intend leading us through
this rare and wonderful new world...

346
00:34:49,339 --> 00:34:52,550
...of indigestion?

347
00:34:52,550 --> 00:34:56,930
They're all right, captain.
They're just happy.

348
00:35:14,155 --> 00:35:16,866
- Rolf, good evening.
- Good evening, Franz.

349
00:35:17,117 --> 00:35:19,828
- I trust everything is under control?
- Yes, yes.

350
00:35:20,078 --> 00:35:21,204
Good.

351
00:35:21,454 --> 00:35:23,873
- Are there any developments?
- Perhaps.

352
00:35:24,124 --> 00:35:26,292
- Is the captain home?
- He's at dinner.

353
00:35:26,543 --> 00:35:28,878
- With the family?
- Yes.

354
00:35:29,129 --> 00:35:31,423
Give him this telegram at once.

355
00:35:31,673 --> 00:35:34,259
Certainly.

356
00:35:44,394 --> 00:35:47,689
A telegram for you, sir.

357
00:35:48,982 --> 00:35:50,859
Franz? Who delivered it?

358
00:35:51,109 --> 00:35:54,696
That young lad Rolf, of course.

359
00:35:55,488 --> 00:35:57,949
- Father, may I be excused?
Hm.

360
00:35:58,199 --> 00:36:02,704
Children, in the morning I shall
be going to Vienna.

361
00:36:02,954 --> 00:36:06,958
Not again, Father!

362
00:36:09,294 --> 00:36:11,755
How long will you be gone this time?

363
00:36:12,005 --> 00:36:13,423
I'm not sure, Gretl.

364
00:36:13,673 --> 00:36:17,969
- To visit Baroness Schraeder again?
- Mind your own business!

365
00:36:18,219 --> 00:36:21,681
As a matter of fact, yes, Louisa.

366
00:36:21,931 --> 00:36:25,769
- Why can't we ever see the baroness?
Why would she want to see you?

367
00:36:26,019 --> 00:36:30,899
You are going to see the baroness.
I'm bringing her back with me to visit.

368
00:36:31,149 --> 00:36:32,192
Good.

369
00:36:32,442 --> 00:36:34,194
And Uncle Max.

370
00:36:34,444 --> 00:36:38,448
Uncle Max!

371
00:36:52,962 --> 00:36:55,799
Rolf!

372
00:36:57,342 --> 00:36:59,928
Oh, Rolf!

373
00:37:00,970 --> 00:37:03,890
- No, Liesl. We mustn't!
- Why not, silly?

374
00:37:04,140 --> 00:37:06,768
- I don't know...
- Isn't this why you're waiting?

375
00:37:07,018 --> 00:37:08,853
Yes, of course.

376
00:37:09,104 --> 00:37:11,898
- I've missed you, Liesl.
- You have? How much?

377
00:37:12,148 --> 00:37:15,985
I even thought of sending a telegram,
so I'd be able to deliver it here.

378
00:37:16,236 --> 00:37:20,115
Oh, that's a lovely thought!
Why don't you, right now?

379
00:37:20,365 --> 00:37:24,536
- But I'm here!
- Please, Rolf. Send me a telegram.

380
00:37:24,786 --> 00:37:28,665
I'll start it for you.
"Dear Liesl..."

381
00:37:28,915 --> 00:37:32,502
"Dear Liesl: I'd like to be able
to tell you...

382
00:37:32,752 --> 00:37:34,963
...how I feel about you. Stop."

383
00:37:35,213 --> 00:37:39,718
"Unfortunately, this wire
is already too expensive."

384
00:37:39,968 --> 00:37:41,344
"Sincerely, Rolf."

385
00:37:41,594 --> 00:37:44,305
- "Sincerely"?
- Cordially.

386
00:37:44,556 --> 00:37:47,851
- "Cordially"?
- Affectionately?

387
00:37:48,101 --> 00:37:49,310
Mmm.

388
00:37:49,561 --> 00:37:52,647
Will there be any reply?

389
00:37:52,897 --> 00:37:55,817
"Dear Rolf: Stop."

390
00:37:56,067 --> 00:38:00,739
"Don't stop! Your Liesl."

391
00:38:00,864 --> 00:38:04,743
If only we didn't have to wait
for someone to send Father a telegram.

392
00:38:04,993 --> 00:38:08,038
How do I know when I'll see you again?

393
00:38:08,288 --> 00:38:11,082
Well, let's see...

394
00:38:12,542 --> 00:38:15,211
I could come here by mistake.

395
00:38:15,462 --> 00:38:20,800
With a telegram for Colonel Schneider!
He's here from Berlin staying with...

396
00:38:21,051 --> 00:38:24,346
No one knows he's here.
Don't tell your father.

397
00:38:24,596 --> 00:38:27,724
- Why not?
- Your father's so Austrian.

398
00:38:27,974 --> 00:38:29,225
We're all Austrian.

399
00:38:29,476 --> 00:38:33,855
Some think we ought to be German, and
they're very mad at those who don't.

400
00:38:34,105 --> 00:38:37,442
They're getting ready to...

401
00:38:37,567 --> 00:38:39,986
Let's hope your father
doesn't get into trouble.

402
00:38:40,236 --> 00:38:45,158
Don't worry. He's a big naval hero.
He was even decorated by the emperor.

403
00:38:45,408 --> 00:38:48,953
I don't worry about him.
I worry about his daughter.

404
00:38:49,204 --> 00:38:50,663
Me? Why?

405
00:38:50,914 --> 00:38:52,665
- Well, you're so...
- What?

406
00:38:52,916 --> 00:38:54,626
You're such a baby!

407
00:38:54,876 --> 00:38:59,339
I'm 16.
What's such a baby about that?

408
00:39:00,048 --> 00:39:03,551
{y:i}You wait, little girl
{y:i}On an empty stage

409
00:39:03,802 --> 00:39:07,555
{y:i}For fate to turn the light on

410
00:39:07,806 --> 00:39:11,059
{y:i}Your life, little girl
{y:i}Is an empty page

411
00:39:11,309 --> 00:39:15,855
{y:i}That men will want to write on

412
00:39:16,106 --> 00:39:22,278
{y:i}To write on

413
00:39:22,529 --> 00:39:26,408
{y:i}You are 16 going on 17

414
00:39:26,658 --> 00:39:30,412
{y:i}Baby, it's time to think

415
00:39:30,662 --> 00:39:34,332
{y:i}Better beware
{y:i}Be canny and careful

416
00:39:34,582 --> 00:39:38,294
{y:i}Baby, you're on the brink

417
00:39:38,545 --> 00:39:42,340
{y:i}You are 16 going on 17

418
00:39:42,590 --> 00:39:46,302
{y:i}Fellows will fall in line

419
00:39:46,553 --> 00:39:49,848
{y:i}Eager young lads
{y:i}And roués and cads

420
00:39:50,098 --> 00:39:54,019
{y:i}Will offer you food and wine

421
00:39:54,269 --> 00:39:58,231
{y:i}Totally unprepared are you

422
00:39:58,481 --> 00:40:02,360
{y:i}To face a world of men

423
00:40:02,610 --> 00:40:06,781
{y:i}Timid and shy and scared are you

424
00:40:07,032 --> 00:40:10,535
{y:i}Of things beyond your ken

425
00:40:10,785 --> 00:40:14,622
{y:i}You need someone older and wiser

426
00:40:14,873 --> 00:40:18,752
{y:i}Telling you what to do

427
00:40:19,002 --> 00:40:22,422
{y:i}I am 17 going on 18

428
00:40:22,672 --> 00:40:30,180
{y:i}I'll take care of you

429
00:41:01,127 --> 00:41:04,464
{y:i}I am 16 going on 17

430
00:41:04,714 --> 00:41:08,093
{y:i}I know that I'm naive

431
00:41:08,343 --> 00:41:11,554
{y:i}Fellows I meet
{y:i}May tell me I'm sweet

432
00:41:11,805 --> 00:41:15,725
{y:i}And willingly I believe

433
00:41:15,975 --> 00:41:19,145
{y:i}I am 16 going on 17

434
00:41:19,396 --> 00:41:23,108
{y:i}Innocent as a rose

435
00:41:23,358 --> 00:41:26,820
{y:i}Bachelor dandies
{y:i}Drinkers of brandies

436
00:41:27,070 --> 00:41:30,407
{y:i}What do I know of those?

437
00:41:30,657 --> 00:41:34,160
{y:i}Totally unprepared am I

438
00:41:34,411 --> 00:41:38,123
{y:i}To face a world of men

439
00:41:38,373 --> 00:41:41,668
{y:i}Timid and shy and scared am I

440
00:41:41,918 --> 00:41:45,839
{y:i}Of things beyond my ken

441
00:41:46,089 --> 00:41:49,676
{y:i}I need someone older and wiser

442
00:41:49,926 --> 00:41:53,346
{y:i}Telling me what to do

443
00:41:53,596 --> 00:41:57,225
{y:i}You are 17 going on 18

444
00:41:57,475 --> 00:42:04,691
{y:i}I'll depend on you

445
00:43:44,958 --> 00:43:48,545
Whee!

446
00:43:56,219 --> 00:43:58,388
Come in.

447
00:43:58,638 --> 00:44:01,850
Frau Schmidt.

448
00:44:02,600 --> 00:44:04,394
For your new dresses.

449
00:44:04,644 --> 00:44:08,690
- The captain had these sent from town.
- Oh, how lovely!

450
00:44:08,940 --> 00:44:12,402
These will make the prettiest
clothes I've ever had.

451
00:44:12,652 --> 00:44:16,156
Do you think he would
get me more material if I asked?

452
00:44:16,406 --> 00:44:20,035
- How many dresses do you need?
- Not for me, for the children.

453
00:44:20,285 --> 00:44:22,328
I want to make them some play clothes.

454
00:44:22,579 --> 00:44:26,374
The von Trapp children don't play.
They march.

455
00:44:26,624 --> 00:44:29,836
Surely you don't approve of that.

456
00:44:30,086 --> 00:44:32,464
Ever since the captain
lost his poor wife...

457
00:44:32,714 --> 00:44:36,009
...he runs this house as if on
one of his ships.

458
00:44:36,259 --> 00:44:37,635
Whistles, orders.

459
00:44:37,886 --> 00:44:39,763
No more music, no more laughing.

460
00:44:40,013 --> 00:44:43,808
Nothing that reminds him of her.
Even the children.

461
00:44:44,059 --> 00:44:45,727
But that's so wrong.

462
00:44:45,977 --> 00:44:47,479
Oh, well.

463
00:44:47,729 --> 00:44:51,399
How do you like your room?
There'll be new drapes at the windows.

464
00:44:51,649 --> 00:44:52,901
Bu these are fine.

465
00:44:53,151 --> 00:44:54,736
New ones have been ordered.

466
00:44:54,986 --> 00:44:58,323
- But I really don't need them.
- Good night, now.

467
00:44:58,573 --> 00:45:02,619
Frau Schmidt, if I asked
the captain about the material...?

468
00:45:02,869 --> 00:45:07,665
- He's leaving in the morning.
- Of course. How long will he be gone?

469
00:45:07,916 --> 00:45:13,421
It depends. The last time he visited
the baroness, he stayed for a month.

470
00:45:13,672 --> 00:45:18,259
I shouldn't be saying this to you.
I don't know you that well.

471
00:45:18,510 --> 00:45:21,388
But if you ask me,
the captain's thinking seriously...

472
00:45:21,638 --> 00:45:23,848
...of marrying her before
summer's over.

473
00:45:24,099 --> 00:45:28,561
Wonderful! The children will
have a mother again.

474
00:45:28,812 --> 00:45:30,105
Yes.

475
00:45:30,355 --> 00:45:32,065
Well, good night.

476
00:45:32,315 --> 00:45:35,276
Good night.

477
00:45:52,127 --> 00:45:54,963
Dear Father, now I know
why You sent me here.

478
00:45:55,213 --> 00:45:58,091
To help these children
prepare for a new mother.

479
00:45:58,341 --> 00:46:01,594
And I pray this will become
a happy family in Thy sight.

480
00:46:01,845 --> 00:46:04,931
God bless the captain.
God bless Liesl and Friedrich.

481
00:46:05,181 --> 00:46:09,394
God bless Louisa, Brigitta,
Marta and little Gretl.

482
00:46:09,644 --> 00:46:14,024
And I forgot the other boy.
What's his name?

483
00:46:14,190 --> 00:46:15,984
Well, God bless what's-his-name.

484
00:46:16,234 --> 00:46:20,280
God bless the Reverend Mother
and Sister Margaretta...

485
00:46:20,530 --> 00:46:22,240
...and everybody at the abbey.

486
00:46:22,490 --> 00:46:25,243
And now, dear God, about Liesl.

487
00:46:25,493 --> 00:46:27,328
Help her know that I'm her friend...

488
00:46:27,579 --> 00:46:30,623
...and help her tell me
what she's been up to.

489
00:46:30,874 --> 00:46:32,083
- Are you going to tell on me?
- Shh.

490
00:46:32,334 --> 00:46:35,754
Help me to be understanding
so I may guide her footsteps.

491
00:46:36,004 --> 00:46:38,631
In the name of the Father,
the Son and the Holy Ghost.

492
00:46:38,882 --> 00:46:41,885
I was out walking
and somebody locked the doors early.

493
00:46:42,135 --> 00:46:47,349
I didn't want to wake everybody,
so when I saw your window open...

494
00:46:47,390 --> 00:46:51,019
You're not going to tell Father,
are you?

495
00:46:51,269 --> 00:46:54,105
Hm.

496
00:46:56,566 --> 00:46:58,109
How did you climb up?

497
00:46:58,360 --> 00:47:01,446
It's how we always got in
to play tricks on the governess.

498
00:47:01,696 --> 00:47:05,450
Louisa can make it with
a whole jar of spiders in her hand.

499
00:47:05,700 --> 00:47:08,119
Spiders?

500
00:47:08,370 --> 00:47:10,830
Oh. Liesl.

501
00:47:11,081 --> 00:47:15,251
Were you out walking all by yourself?

502
00:47:21,174 --> 00:47:25,303
If we wash that dress tonight,
nobody would notice it tomorrow.

503
00:47:25,553 --> 00:47:28,890
You could put this on.

504
00:47:29,057 --> 00:47:31,726
Take your dress and put it
to soak in the bathtub.

505
00:47:31,976 --> 00:47:36,564
Come back here and sit on the bed,
and we'll have a talk.

506
00:47:36,815 --> 00:47:39,818
I told you today
I didn't need a governess.

507
00:47:40,068 --> 00:47:43,697
Well, maybe I do.

508
00:48:12,642 --> 00:48:15,103
Gretl, are you scared?

509
00:48:18,690 --> 00:48:20,942
You're not frightened
of a storm, are you?

510
00:48:21,192 --> 00:48:24,404
You just stay right here with me.

511
00:48:24,654 --> 00:48:30,410
- Where are the others?
- They're asleep. They're not scared.

512
00:48:33,288 --> 00:48:36,666
Oh, no? Look.

513
00:48:36,666 --> 00:48:38,626
All right, up here on the bed.

514
00:48:38,877 --> 00:48:42,464
- Really?
- Well, just this once. Come on.

515
00:48:42,714 --> 00:48:48,595
- Now we'll wait for the boys.
- You won't see them. Boys are brave.

516
00:48:53,141 --> 00:48:55,185
You weren't scared, were you?

517
00:48:55,435 --> 00:48:59,522
Oh, no. We just wanted
to be sure that you weren't.

518
00:48:59,773 --> 00:49:02,817
- That's very thoughtful of you.
It wasn't my idea.

519
00:49:03,068 --> 00:49:04,903
It was Kurt's!

520
00:49:05,153 --> 00:49:09,324
Kurt! That's the one I left out!
God bless Kurt.

521
00:49:10,575 --> 00:49:12,327
Why does it do that?

522
00:49:12,577 --> 00:49:16,414
The lightning talks to the thunder,
and the thunder answers.

523
00:49:16,664 --> 00:49:19,167
- But lightning must be nasty.
- Not really.

524
00:49:19,417 --> 00:49:21,670
Why does the thunder get so angry?

525
00:49:21,920 --> 00:49:25,674
It makes me want to cry.

526
00:49:25,965 --> 00:49:30,136
Whenever I'm feeling unhappy,
I just try to think of nice things.

527
00:49:30,387 --> 00:49:32,722
What kind of things?

528
00:49:32,972 --> 00:49:34,933
Well, let me see. Nice things...

529
00:49:35,183 --> 00:49:37,268
Daffodils.

530
00:49:37,519 --> 00:49:39,104
Green meadows.

531
00:49:39,354 --> 00:49:41,564
Skies full of stars.

532
00:49:41,815 --> 00:49:44,984
{y:i}Raindrops on roses
{y:i}and whiskers on kittens

533
00:49:45,235 --> 00:49:48,321
{y:i}Bright copper kettles
{y:i}And warm woolen mittens

534
00:49:48,571 --> 00:49:51,741
{y:i}Brown paper packages
{y:i}Tied up with strings

535
00:49:51,991 --> 00:49:57,539
{y:i}These are a few of my favorite things

536
00:49:58,581 --> 00:50:01,376
{y:i}Cream-colored ponies
{y:i}And crisp apple strudels

537
00:50:01,626 --> 00:50:04,838
{y:i}Doorbells and sleigh bells
{y:i}And schnitzel with noodles

538
00:50:05,088 --> 00:50:08,008
{y:i}Wild geese that fly
{y:i}With the moon on their wings

539
00:50:08,258 --> 00:50:13,096
{y:i}These are a few of my favorite things

540
00:50:13,346 --> 00:50:16,099
{y:i}Girls in white dresses
{y:i}With blue satin sashes

541
00:50:16,349 --> 00:50:19,561
{y:i}Snowflakes that stay
{y:i}On my nose and eyelashes

542
00:50:19,811 --> 00:50:22,897
{y:i}Silver white winters
{y:i}That melt into springs

543
00:50:23,148 --> 00:50:26,484
{y:i}These are a few of my favorite things

544
00:50:26,735 --> 00:50:28,361
{y:i}When the dog bites

545
00:50:28,611 --> 00:50:30,321
{y:i}When the bee stings

546
00:50:30,572 --> 00:50:32,824
{y:i}When I'm feeling sad

547
00:50:33,074 --> 00:50:36,453
{y:i}I simply remember my favorite things

548
00:50:36,703 --> 00:50:44,002
{y:i}And then I don't feel so bad

549
00:50:44,252 --> 00:50:45,962
- Does it work?
- Of course.

550
00:50:46,212 --> 00:50:48,631
- What do you like?
- Pussy willow!

551
00:50:48,882 --> 00:50:51,092
- Christmas!
- Bunny rabbits!

552
00:50:51,343 --> 00:50:53,053
Snakes!

553
00:50:53,303 --> 00:50:55,430
- Chocolate icing!
- No school!

554
00:50:55,680 --> 00:50:56,890
Pillow fights!

555
00:50:57,140 --> 00:50:59,267
- Telegrams!
- Birthday presents!

556
00:50:59,517 --> 00:51:03,396
- Any presents!
Ladybugs!

557
00:51:03,396 --> 00:51:04,731
A good sneeze!

558
00:51:04,981 --> 00:51:06,775
Gesundheit!

559
00:51:07,025 --> 00:51:08,276
See what fun it is?

560
00:51:08,526 --> 00:51:11,488
{y:i}Raindrops on roses
{y:i}And whiskers on kittens

561
00:51:11,738 --> 00:51:14,616
{y:i}Bright copper kettles
{y:i}And warm woolen mittens

562
00:51:14,866 --> 00:51:17,869
{y:i}Brown paper packages
{y:i}Tied up with strings

563
00:51:18,119 --> 00:51:22,499
{y:i}These are a few of my favorite things

564
00:51:24,376 --> 00:51:27,212
{y:i}Cream-colored ponies
{y:i}And crisp apple strudels

565
00:51:27,462 --> 00:51:30,298
{y:i}Doorbells and sleigh bells
{y:i}And schnitzel with noodles

566
00:51:30,548 --> 00:51:33,510
{y:i}Wild geese that fly
{y:i}With the moon on their wings

567
00:51:33,760 --> 00:51:37,972
{y:i}These are a few of my favorite things

568
00:51:38,223 --> 00:51:39,766
Together.

569
00:51:40,016 --> 00:51:42,769
{y:i}Girls in white dresses
{y:i}With blue satin sashes

570
00:51:43,019 --> 00:51:45,897
{y:i}Snowflakes that stay
{y:i}On my nose and eyelashes

571
00:51:46,147 --> 00:51:49,150
{y:i}Silver white winters
{y:i}That melt into springs

572
00:51:49,401 --> 00:51:52,696
{y:i}These are a few of my favorite things

573
00:51:52,946 --> 00:51:55,073
{y:i}When the dog bi...

574
00:51:55,323 --> 00:51:58,785
Dog bites.

575
00:52:03,915 --> 00:52:05,125
Hello.

576
00:52:05,375 --> 00:52:09,546
Fräulein, did I not tell you that
bedtime is to be strictly observed?

577
00:52:09,796 --> 00:52:13,633
The children were upset
by the storm, so I...

578
00:52:13,883 --> 00:52:14,884
You did, sir.

579
00:52:15,135 --> 00:52:20,390
Do you, or do you not, have difficulty
remembering such simple instructions?

580
00:52:20,640 --> 00:52:22,308
Only during thunderstorms.

581
00:52:24,769 --> 00:52:26,229
- Liesl?
- Yes?

582
00:52:26,479 --> 00:52:29,065
I don't recall seeing you
after dinner.

583
00:52:29,315 --> 00:52:31,067
Really? As a matter of fact...

584
00:52:31,317 --> 00:52:33,111
Yes?

585
00:52:33,361 --> 00:52:34,487
Well, I was...

586
00:52:34,738 --> 00:52:36,823
What she would like to say...

587
00:52:37,073 --> 00:52:39,909
...is that she and I have
been getting acquainted tonight.

588
00:52:40,160 --> 00:52:46,541
It's too late to go into that. You
heard your father. Go back to bed.

589
00:52:49,669 --> 00:52:51,087
Fräulein...

590
00:52:51,338 --> 00:52:56,217
...you have managed to remember
I'm leaving in the morning?

591
00:52:56,468 --> 00:53:02,599
Is it also possible you remember the
first rule in this house is discipline?

592
00:53:02,724 --> 00:53:05,435
Then I trust that before I return...

593
00:53:05,685 --> 00:53:08,605
...you'll have acquired some?

594
00:53:08,855 --> 00:53:10,899
Captain?

595
00:53:11,149 --> 00:53:15,070
Could I talk to you about clothes
for the children for when they play?

596
00:53:15,320 --> 00:53:18,073
- If I could have some material.
- You are many things.

597
00:53:18,323 --> 00:53:20,408
Not the least of which is repetitious.

598
00:53:20,658 --> 00:53:22,869
- But they're children!
- Yes.

599
00:53:23,119 --> 00:53:25,205
And I'm their father.

600
00:53:25,455 --> 00:53:28,208
Good night.

601
00:53:29,501 --> 00:53:32,629
Oh.

602
00:53:50,980 --> 00:53:58,321
{y:i}Girls in white dresses
{y:i}With blue satin sashes

603
00:54:08,289 --> 00:54:11,793
{y:i}When the dog bites
{y:i}When the bee stings

604
00:54:12,043 --> 00:54:14,504
{y:i}When I'm feeling sad

605
00:54:14,754 --> 00:54:18,049
{y:i}I simply remember my favorite things

606
00:54:18,299 --> 00:54:20,427
{y:i}And then I don't feel...

607
00:54:20,677 --> 00:54:29,144
{y:i}... so bad

608
00:54:51,583 --> 00:54:55,837
Children, over here. See!

609
00:54:57,922 --> 00:55:00,842
Come on.

610
00:55:27,869 --> 00:55:30,663
Ho.

611
00:56:22,340 --> 00:56:23,591
Fräulein Maria?

612
00:56:23,842 --> 00:56:25,760
Can we do this every day?

613
00:56:26,011 --> 00:56:29,347
- Don't you think you'd get tired of it?
- I suppose so.

614
00:56:29,597 --> 00:56:30,807
Every other day?

615
00:56:31,057 --> 00:56:35,645
I haven't had so much fun since we put
glue on Fräulein Josephine's toothbrush.

616
00:56:35,895 --> 00:56:39,816
I can't understand how children
as nice as you can play such tricks.

617
00:56:40,066 --> 00:56:41,860
- It's easy.
- But why do it?

618
00:56:42,110 --> 00:56:45,071
How else can we get
Father's attention?

619
00:56:45,321 --> 00:56:47,991
Oh, I see.

620
00:56:48,241 --> 00:56:51,995
We'll have to think about that one.

621
00:56:52,620 --> 00:56:54,247
All right, over here.

622
00:56:54,497 --> 00:56:55,874
What are we going to do?

623
00:56:56,124 --> 00:56:58,084
Think of a song for the baroness.

624
00:56:58,335 --> 00:57:00,295
Father doesn't like us to sing.

625
00:57:00,545 --> 00:57:04,132
Perhaps we can change his mind.
Now, what songs do you know?

626
00:57:04,382 --> 00:57:06,509
We don't know any songs.

627
00:57:06,760 --> 00:57:11,014
- Not any?
- We don't even know how to sing.

628
00:57:11,181 --> 00:57:13,308
Let's not lose time. You must learn.

629
00:57:13,558 --> 00:57:16,811
But how?

630
00:57:19,606 --> 00:57:23,526
{y:i}Let's start at the very beginning

631
00:57:23,777 --> 00:57:27,113
{y:i}A very good place to start

632
00:57:27,364 --> 00:57:29,282
{y:i}When you read you begin with

633
00:57:29,532 --> 00:57:30,658
{y:i}A, B, C

634
00:57:30,909 --> 00:57:35,288
{y:i}When you sing, you begin with
{y:i}Do-Re-Mi

635
00:57:35,538 --> 00:57:37,165
{y:i}Do-re-mi

636
00:57:37,415 --> 00:57:38,750
{y:i}Do-re-mi

637
00:57:39,000 --> 00:57:42,837
{y:i}The first three notes
{y:i}Just happen to be

638
00:57:43,088 --> 00:57:44,839
{y:i}Do-re-mi

639
00:57:45,090 --> 00:57:46,383
{y:i}Do-re-mi

640
00:57:46,633 --> 00:57:50,970
{y:i}Do-re-mi-fa-so-la-ti

641
00:57:51,221 --> 00:57:55,183
Let's see if I can make it easier.

642
00:57:57,018 --> 00:58:00,689
{y:i}"Doe", a deer
{y:i}A female deer

643
00:58:00,939 --> 00:58:04,526
{y:i}"Ray", a drop of golden sun

644
00:58:04,776 --> 00:58:08,571
{y:i}"Me", a name I call myself

645
00:58:08,822 --> 00:58:12,534
{y:i}"Far", a long long way to run

646
00:58:12,784 --> 00:58:16,579
{y:i}"Sew", a needle pulling thread

647
00:58:16,830 --> 00:58:20,667
{y:i}"La", a note to follow sew

648
00:58:20,917 --> 00:58:24,129
{y:i}"Tea", a drink with jam and bread

649
00:58:24,379 --> 00:58:28,717
{y:i}That will bring us back to doe

650
00:58:28,967 --> 00:58:32,554
{y:i}- Doe
{y:i}- A deer, a female deer

651
00:58:32,804 --> 00:58:36,016
{y:i}- Ray
{y:i}- A drop of golden sun

652
