705
01:01:54,923 --> 01:01:58,093
{y:i}Doe
{y:i}A deer, a female deer

706
01:01:58,343 --> 01:02:01,846
{y:i}Ray
{y:i}A drop of golden sun

707
01:02:02,097 --> 01:02:05,558
{y:i}Me
{y:i}A name I call myself

708
01:02:05,809 --> 01:02:08,937
{y:i}Far
{y:i}A long long way to run

709
01:02:09,187 --> 01:02:12,357
{y:i}Sew
{y:i}A needle pulling thread

710
01:02:12,607 --> 01:02:16,111
{y:i}La
{y:i}A note to follow sew

711
01:02:16,361 --> 01:02:19,030
{y:i}Tea
{y:i}A drink with jam and bread

712
01:02:19,280 --> 01:02:23,201
{y:i}That will bring us back to

713
01:02:23,451 --> 01:02:30,291
{y:i}So-do-la-fa-mi-do-re

714
01:02:30,542 --> 01:02:35,005
{y:i}So-do-la-fa-ti

715
01:02:35,255 --> 01:02:38,466
{y:i}La-so

716
01:02:40,427 --> 01:02:46,224
{y:i}Ti-do

717
01:02:46,474 --> 01:02:49,477
{y:i}So-do

718
01:03:01,406 --> 01:03:04,492
The mountains are magnificent,
really magnificent.

719
01:03:04,743 --> 01:03:07,412
- I had them put up just for you.
- Oh?

720
01:03:07,662 --> 01:03:09,873
Even if it's to a height
of 10,000 feet...

721
01:03:10,123 --> 01:03:13,626
...Georg always believes in
"rising to the occasion".

722
01:03:13,877 --> 01:03:16,546
Improve the jokes
or I'll disinvite you.

723
01:03:16,796 --> 01:03:19,382
You didn't invite me.
I invited myself.

724
01:03:19,632 --> 01:03:22,177
- Naturally.
- You needed a chaperone...

725
01:03:22,427 --> 01:03:25,138
...and I needed a place
where the cuisine is superb...

726
01:03:25,388 --> 01:03:28,058
...the wine cellar unexcelled...

727
01:03:28,308 --> 01:03:31,061
...and the price perfect.

728
01:03:31,311 --> 01:03:33,146
Max, you are outrageous.

729
01:03:33,396 --> 01:03:37,359
Not at all.
I'm a very charming sponge.

730
01:03:39,986 --> 01:03:42,697
Listen.

731
01:03:43,490 --> 01:03:47,285
That's the Klopmann Monastery Choir.

732
01:03:48,745 --> 01:03:51,164
They're good.

733
01:03:51,414 --> 01:03:53,583
Very good.

734
01:03:53,833 --> 01:03:56,419
I must explore this area
in the next few days.

735
01:03:56,670 --> 01:04:00,173
Somewhere, a hungry singing group
is waiting for Max Detweiler...

736
01:04:00,423 --> 01:04:04,678
...to pluck it out and make it famous
at the Salzburg Folk Festival.

737
01:04:04,928 --> 01:04:08,223
- They get fame, you get money.
- It's unfair, I admit it.

738
01:04:08,473 --> 01:04:12,977
But someday that'll be changed.
I shall get the fame too.

739
01:04:19,901 --> 01:04:23,238
Good heavens, what's this?

740
01:04:23,488 --> 01:04:27,325
It's nothing.
Just some local urchins.

741
01:04:41,047 --> 01:04:45,510
This really is exciting for me, Georg.
Being here with you.

742
01:04:45,760 --> 01:04:48,346
Trees, lakes,
you've seen them before.

743
01:04:48,596 --> 01:04:51,224
That is not what I mean,
and you know it.

744
01:04:51,474 --> 01:04:55,437
- You mean me? I'm exciting?
- Is that so impossible?

745
01:04:55,687 --> 01:04:58,732
No, just highly improbable.

746
01:04:58,982 --> 01:05:04,654
- There you go, running yourself down.
- Well, I'm a dangerous driver.

747
01:05:06,906 --> 01:05:10,577
You're much less of a riddle
when I see you here, Georg.

748
01:05:10,827 --> 01:05:13,663
- In my natural habitat?
- Yes, exactly.

749
01:05:13,913 --> 01:05:16,082
Are you saying that I'm more
at home...

750
01:05:16,332 --> 01:05:20,337
...among the birds and the flowers
and the wind that moves...

751
01:05:20,587 --> 01:05:22,756
...through the trees
like a restless sea?

752
01:05:23,006 --> 01:05:24,549
How poetic.

753
01:05:24,799 --> 01:05:27,552
Yes, it was rather, wasn't it?

754
01:05:27,802 --> 01:05:32,891
More at home here than in Vienna
in all your glittering salons...

755
01:05:33,141 --> 01:05:37,520
...gossiping gaily with bores I
detest, soaking myself in champagne...

756
01:05:37,771 --> 01:05:41,524
...stumbling about to waltzes
by Strausses I can't even remember?

757
01:05:41,775 --> 01:05:43,902
- Is that what you're saying?
- Yes.

758
01:05:44,152 --> 01:05:48,031
Now whatever gave you that idea?

759
01:05:49,866 --> 01:05:53,828
Oh, I do like it here, Georg.
It's so lovely and peaceful.

760
01:05:54,079 --> 01:05:56,039
How can you leave it so often?

761
01:05:56,289 --> 01:05:59,125
Oh, pretending to be madly active,
I suppose.

762
01:05:59,376 --> 01:06:03,129
Activity suggests a life
filled with purpose.

763
01:06:03,380 --> 01:06:06,758
Could it be running away
from memories?

764
01:06:07,008 --> 01:06:08,593
Mm-hm.

765
01:06:08,843 --> 01:06:13,181
Or perhaps just searching
for a reason to stay.

766
01:06:13,431 --> 01:06:17,352
I hope that's why you've been
coming to Vienna so often.

767
01:06:17,602 --> 01:06:21,940
- Were there other distractions?
- I'd hardly call you a distraction.

768
01:06:22,190 --> 01:06:26,403
Well, what would you call me, Georg?

769
01:06:27,112 --> 01:06:29,155
Lovely...

770
01:06:29,406 --> 01:06:31,991
...charming, witty, graceful,
the perfect hostess...

771
01:06:32,242 --> 01:06:35,245
...and, you're going to hate me
for this...

772
01:06:35,495 --> 01:06:37,580
...in a way, my savior.

773
01:06:37,831 --> 01:06:39,624
Oh, how unromantic.

774
01:06:39,874 --> 01:06:42,210
I'd be an ungrateful wretch
if I didn't say...

775
01:06:42,460 --> 01:06:47,257
...that you brought some meaning back
into my life.

776
01:06:47,549 --> 01:06:50,260
I am amusing, I suppose.

777
01:06:50,510 --> 01:06:55,265
I have the finest couturier in Vienna
and a glittering circle of friends.

778
01:06:55,515 --> 01:06:58,643
- I do give some rather gay parties.
- Oh, yes.

779
01:06:58,893 --> 01:07:00,687
But take all that away...

780
01:07:00,937 --> 01:07:05,775
...and you have just wealthy,
unattached little me...

781
01:07:06,026 --> 01:07:08,862
...searching, just like you.

782
01:07:13,533 --> 01:07:15,243
More strudel, Herr Detweiler?

783
01:07:15,493 --> 01:07:17,037
- How many have I had?
- Two.

784
01:07:17,287 --> 01:07:20,749
Make it an uneven three.

785
01:07:22,500 --> 01:07:25,503
Still eating, Max?
Must be unhappy.

786
01:07:25,754 --> 01:07:29,716
That mixed quartet I've been trying
to steal away from Sol Hurok...

787
01:07:29,966 --> 01:07:32,594
- What happened?
...Sascha Petrie stole them first.

788
01:07:32,844 --> 01:07:34,137
I hate thieves.

789
01:07:34,387 --> 01:07:37,682
Max, you really must try
and learn to love yourself.

790
01:07:37,932 --> 01:07:40,268
I had to call Paris, Rome
and Stockholm.

791
01:07:40,518 --> 01:07:44,314
- On Georg's telephone, of course.
- How else could I afford it?

792
01:07:44,564 --> 01:07:49,736
I like rich people, the way they live
and how I live when I'm with them.

793
01:07:49,986 --> 01:07:52,155
I wonder where the children are.

794
01:07:52,405 --> 01:07:54,949
They must have heard
I was coming and hid.

795
01:07:55,200 --> 01:07:57,702
I was hoping they'd be here
to welcome you.

796
01:07:57,952 --> 01:08:04,000
Max, do step out of character for
a moment and try and be charming.

797
01:08:11,508 --> 01:08:13,551
Well?

798
01:08:13,802 --> 01:08:14,969
Well what?

799
01:08:15,220 --> 01:08:18,139
Have you made up his mind?
Do I hear wedding bells?

800
01:08:18,390 --> 01:08:20,392
- Pealing madly.
- Marvelous.

801
01:08:20,642 --> 01:08:23,311
- Not necessarily for me.
- What kind of talk's that?

802
01:08:23,561 --> 01:08:25,188
None-of-your-business talk.

803
01:08:25,438 --> 01:08:27,941
I'm terribly fond of him,
so don't toy with us.

804
01:08:28,191 --> 01:08:31,778
But I'm a child. I like toys.
So tell me everything.

805
01:08:32,028 --> 01:08:38,368
Come on. Tell me every teensy-weensy,
intimate, disgusting detail.

806
01:08:38,618 --> 01:08:42,747
Well, let's just say I have a feeling
I may be here on approval.

807
01:08:42,997 --> 01:08:46,042
- I approve of that. How can you miss?
- Far too easily.

808
01:08:46,292 --> 01:08:49,504
If I know you, darling,
and I do, you will find a way.

809
01:08:49,754 --> 01:08:52,298
- He's no ordinary man.
- No, he's rich.

810
01:08:52,549 --> 01:08:54,759
His wife's death gave him
a great heartache.

811
01:08:55,010 --> 01:08:58,096
And your husband's death
gave you a great fortune.

812
01:08:58,346 --> 01:09:00,265
Oh, Max, you really are a beast.

813
01:09:00,515 --> 01:09:04,519
You and Georg are like family.
That's why I want to see you married.

814
01:09:04,769 --> 01:09:09,899
We must keep all that lovely money
in the family.

815
01:09:24,622 --> 01:09:27,584
- What are you doing there?
- Oh, Captain von Trapp.

816
01:09:27,834 --> 01:09:30,503
I was just looking for...

817
01:09:30,754 --> 01:09:33,923
I didn't see, I mean,
I didn't know you were...

818
01:09:34,174 --> 01:09:36,343
Heil Hitler!

819
01:09:36,593 --> 01:09:38,094
Who are you?

820
01:09:38,345 --> 01:09:40,555
I have a telegram for Herr Detweiler.

821
01:09:40,805 --> 01:09:45,143
- I am Herr Detweiler.
- Yes, sir.

822
01:09:48,646 --> 01:09:52,859
You've delivered your telegram.
Now get out.

823
01:09:58,031 --> 01:10:01,368
- Georg, he's just a boy.
- Yes, and I'm just an Austrian.

824
01:10:01,618 --> 01:10:04,454
Things will happen.
Make sure they don't happen to you.

825
01:10:04,704 --> 01:10:07,415
Max! Don't you ever say that again.

826
01:10:07,665 --> 01:10:09,876
I have no political convictions.

827
01:10:10,126 --> 01:10:13,880
- Can I help it if other people do?
- You can help it.

828
01:10:14,130 --> 01:10:17,884
You must help it.

829
01:10:26,643 --> 01:10:29,771
Hello?

830
01:10:30,814 --> 01:10:33,692
You're far away. Where are you?

831
01:10:33,942 --> 01:10:37,237
In a world that's disappearing,
I'm afraid.

832
01:10:38,655 --> 01:10:43,743
Is there any way I could bring you
back to the world I'm in?

833
01:10:59,300 --> 01:11:03,096
{y:i}And that will bring us back to doe

834
01:11:03,346 --> 01:11:05,932
{y:i}A deer, a female deer

835
01:11:08,143 --> 01:11:11,187
- Father! Father!
- There's your father!

836
01:11:11,438 --> 01:11:15,650
Oh, captain, you're home!

837
01:11:22,490 --> 01:11:26,703
Come out of that water at once!

838
01:11:34,210 --> 01:11:37,964
Oh, you must be Baroness Schraeder.

839
01:11:41,718 --> 01:11:46,306
I'm soaked to the skin!

840
01:11:56,733 --> 01:11:59,652
Straight line!

841
01:12:12,791 --> 01:12:16,753
This is Baroness Schraeder.

842
01:12:17,796 --> 01:12:19,547
And these...

843
01:12:19,798 --> 01:12:22,217
...are my children.

844
01:12:22,467 --> 01:12:24,844
How do you do?

845
01:12:25,095 --> 01:12:31,351
Go inside, dry off, clean up, change
your clothes and report back here!

846
01:12:34,813 --> 01:12:38,441
Fräulein, you will stay here, please!

847
01:12:38,650 --> 01:12:43,738
I think I'd better go see
what Max is up to.

848
01:12:52,414 --> 01:12:55,750
Now, fräulein...

849
01:12:56,126 --> 01:12:58,336
...I want a truthful answer.

850
01:12:58,586 --> 01:13:00,088
Yes, captain.

851
01:13:00,338 --> 01:13:05,677
Is it possible,
or could I have just imagined it?

852
01:13:05,927 --> 01:13:10,640
Have my children, by any chance,
been climbing trees today?

853
01:13:10,890 --> 01:13:12,892
Yes, captain.

854
01:13:13,143 --> 01:13:14,769
I see.

855
01:13:15,020 --> 01:13:19,524
And where, may I ask,
did they get these...

856
01:13:19,774 --> 01:13:21,818
- Play clothes.
- Is that what they are?

857
01:13:22,068 --> 01:13:26,031
I made them from the drapes
that used to hang in my bedroom.

858
01:13:26,281 --> 01:13:28,366
- Drapes?
- They have plenty of wear left.

859
01:13:28,616 --> 01:13:30,535
We've been everywhere in them.

860
01:13:30,785 --> 01:13:35,248
Are you telling me that my children
have been roaming about Salzburg...

861
01:13:35,498 --> 01:13:38,793
...dressed up in nothing
but some old drapes?

862
01:13:39,044 --> 01:13:40,670
And having a marvelous time!

863
01:13:40,920 --> 01:13:44,132
- They have uniforms.
- Forgive me, straitjackets.

864
01:13:44,382 --> 01:13:47,177
They can't be children
if they worry about clothes...

865
01:13:47,427 --> 01:13:48,553
They don't complain.

866
01:13:48,803 --> 01:13:51,473
They don't dare.
They love you too much and fear...

867
01:13:51,723 --> 01:13:53,266
Don't discuss my children.

868
01:13:53,516 --> 01:13:55,393
You've got to hear, you're
never home...

869
01:13:55,643 --> 01:13:57,103
I don't want to hear more!

870
01:13:57,354 --> 01:14:00,440
I know you don't, but you've got to!

871
01:14:00,690 --> 01:14:02,859
- Liesl's not a child.
- Not one word...

872
01:14:03,109 --> 01:14:05,987
Soon she'll be a woman
and you won't even know her.

873
01:14:06,237 --> 01:14:09,324
Friedrich wants to be a man
but you're not here to show...

874
01:14:09,574 --> 01:14:10,909
Don't you dare tell me...

875
01:14:11,159 --> 01:14:14,412
Brigitta could tell you about him.
She notices everything.

876
01:14:14,662 --> 01:14:17,749
Kurt acts tough to hide the pain
when you ignore him...

877
01:14:17,999 --> 01:14:19,542
...the way you do all of them.

878
01:14:19,793 --> 01:14:22,212
Louisa, I don't know about yet.

879
01:14:22,462 --> 01:14:25,674
The little ones just want love.
Please, love them all.

880
01:14:25,924 --> 01:14:27,467
I don't care to hear more.

881
01:14:27,717 --> 01:14:33,348
- I am not finished yet, captain!
- Oh, yes, you are, captain!

882
01:14:33,348 --> 01:14:35,517
Fräulein.

883
01:14:35,767 --> 01:14:39,896
Now, you will pack
your things this minute...

884
01:14:40,146 --> 01:14:42,607
...and return to the abbey.

885
01:14:45,235 --> 01:14:48,154
What's that?

886
01:14:48,321 --> 01:14:49,781
It's singing.

887
01:14:50,031 --> 01:14:53,535
Yes, I realize it's singing.
But who is singing?

888
01:14:53,785 --> 01:14:56,913
The children.

889
01:14:58,248 --> 01:14:59,874
The children?

890
01:15:00,125 --> 01:15:04,421
I taught them something to sing
for the baroness.

891
01:15:08,508 --> 01:15:14,556
{y:i}My heart wants to sing
{y:i}Every song it hears

892
01:15:14,806 --> 01:15:17,392
{y:i}Every song that it hears

893
01:15:17,642 --> 01:15:23,106
{y:i}My heart wants to beat like the wings
{y:i}Of the birds that rise

894
01:15:23,356 --> 01:15:26,484
{y:i}From the lake to the trees

895
01:15:26,735 --> 01:15:28,153
{y:i}To the trees

896
01:15:28,403 --> 01:15:33,283
{y:i}My heart wants to sigh
{y:i}Like a chime that flies

897
01:15:33,533 --> 01:15:38,163
{y:i}From a church on a breeze

898
01:15:38,413 --> 01:15:42,334
{y:i}To laugh like a brook
{y:i}When it trips and falls

899
01:15:42,584 --> 01:15:45,545
{y:i}Over stones on its way

900
01:15:45,795 --> 01:15:47,213
{y:i}On its way

901
01:15:47,464 --> 01:15:51,843
{y:i}To sing through the night

902
01:15:52,093 --> 01:15:57,766
{y:i}Like a lark who is learning to pray

903
01:15:58,016 --> 01:16:02,479
{y:i}I go to the hills

904
01:16:02,729 --> 01:16:08,985
{y:i}When my heart is lonely

905
01:16:09,235 --> 01:16:12,947
{y:i}I know I will hear

906
01:16:13,198 --> 01:16:19,537
{y:i}What I've heard before

907
01:16:19,788 --> 01:16:24,042
{y:i}My heart will be blessed

908
01:16:24,292 --> 01:16:31,883
{y:i}With the sound of music

909
01:16:32,133 --> 01:16:37,305
{y:i}And I'll sing...

910
01:16:37,555 --> 01:16:47,232
{y:i}... once more

911
01:17:18,930 --> 01:17:22,267
Edelweiss!

912
01:17:24,644 --> 01:17:29,357
You never told me how enchanting
your children are.

913
01:17:36,448 --> 01:17:39,576
Don't go away.

914
01:17:43,121 --> 01:17:46,041
Fräulein.

915
01:17:47,876 --> 01:17:50,253
I...

916
01:17:50,503 --> 01:17:54,883
...behaved badly. I apologize.

917
01:17:55,133 --> 01:17:58,845
I'm far too outspoken.
It's one of my worst faults.

918
01:17:59,095 --> 01:18:02,098
You were right.

919
01:18:03,016 --> 01:18:05,977
I don't know my children.

920
01:18:06,227 --> 01:18:10,398
There's still time, captain.
They want so much to be close to you.

921
01:18:10,648 --> 01:18:15,612
And you brought music back
into the house.

922
01:18:15,862 --> 01:18:18,865
I'd forgotten.

923
01:18:20,033 --> 01:18:22,952
Fräulein.

924
01:18:23,870 --> 01:18:26,706
I want you to stay.

925
01:18:29,167 --> 01:18:33,463
I ask you to stay.

926
01:18:33,755 --> 01:18:37,592
- If I could be of any help.
- You have already.

927
01:18:37,842 --> 01:18:40,428
More than you know.

928
01:18:58,613 --> 01:19:00,407
Marta.

929
01:19:00,657 --> 01:19:03,493
Curtain!

930
01:19:04,577 --> 01:19:09,457
{y:i}High on a hill was a lonely goatherd

931
01:19:09,457 --> 01:19:13,920
{y:i}Loud was the voice
{y:i}Of the lonely goatherd

932
01:19:14,170 --> 01:19:19,050
{y:i}Folks in a town
{y:i}That was quite remote heard

933
01:19:19,050 --> 01:19:23,847
{y:i}Lusty and clear
{y:i}From the goatherd's throat heard

934
01:19:33,815 --> 01:19:38,236
Marta. Marta!

935
01:19:39,070 --> 01:19:42,615
Gretl, the prince!

936
01:19:42,824 --> 01:19:47,454
{y:i}A prince on the bridge
{y:i}Of a castle moat heard

937
01:19:47,704 --> 01:19:51,624
{y:i}Men on a road
{y:i}With a load to tote heard

938
01:19:52,584 --> 01:19:56,963
{y:i}Men in the midst
{y:i}Of a table d'hôte heard

939
01:19:57,213 --> 01:20:00,717
{y:i}Men drinking beer
{y:i}With the foam afloat heard

940
01:20:11,519 --> 01:20:16,191
{y:i}One little girl
{y:i}In a pale pink coat heard

941
01:20:16,316 --> 01:20:20,528
{y:i}She yodeled back
{y:i}To the lonely goatherd

942
01:20:20,779 --> 01:20:25,492
{y:i}Soon her mama
{y:i}With a gleaming gloat heard

943
01:20:25,742 --> 01:20:30,580
{y:i}What a duet for a girl and goatherd

944
01:21:15,625 --> 01:21:20,130
{y:i}One little girl
{y:i}In a pale pink coat heard

945
01:21:20,380 --> 01:21:24,759
{y:i}She yodeled back
{y:i}To the lonely goatherd

946
01:21:25,010 --> 01:21:29,639
{y:i}Soon her mama
{y:i}With a gleaming gloat heard

947
01:21:29,889 --> 01:21:34,602
{y:i}What a duet for a girl and goatherd

948
01:21:34,728 --> 01:21:36,354
{y:i}Happy are they

949
01:21:39,399 --> 01:21:43,737
{y:i}Soon the duet will become a trio

950
01:22:07,135 --> 01:22:08,678
- Bravo!
- Bravo!

951
01:22:08,928 --> 01:22:10,513
Very good!

952
01:22:10,764 --> 01:22:11,973
- Bravo!
- Bravo!

953
01:22:12,223 --> 01:22:14,476
Wonderful!

954
01:22:14,726 --> 01:22:17,854
- Can we keep the puppets, Uncle Max?
Yes, can we?

955
01:22:18,104 --> 01:22:20,106
Of course you may, my darlings.

956
01:22:20,357 --> 01:22:26,112
Why else did I tell Professor Kohner
to send the bill to your father?

957
01:22:28,281 --> 01:22:31,534
Well done, fräulein.

958
01:22:32,494 --> 01:22:34,412
I really am very much impressed.

959
01:22:34,662 --> 01:22:38,208
They're your children, captain.

960
01:22:43,838 --> 01:22:46,174
My dear, is there anything
you can't do?

961
01:22:46,424 --> 01:22:48,718
Well, I'm not sure
I'll make a good nun.

962
01:22:48,968 --> 01:22:53,640
If you have any problems,
I'd be happy to help you.

963
01:22:55,225 --> 01:22:56,601
Attention, everyone!

964
01:22:56,851 --> 01:23:00,271
I have an announcement to make.
Surprise! Surprise!

965
01:23:00,522 --> 01:23:02,857
Today, after a long
and desperate search...

966
01:23:03,108 --> 01:23:08,071
...I have found a most exciting
entry for the Salzburg Folk Festival.

967
01:23:08,321 --> 01:23:10,156
Congratulations, Max.

968
01:23:10,407 --> 01:23:14,911
And who will you
be exploiting this time?

969
01:23:15,120 --> 01:23:18,832
- The Saint Ignatius Choir?
- Guess again.

970
01:23:19,082 --> 01:23:23,003
Well, let me see now.
The Klopmann Choir?

971
01:23:23,253 --> 01:23:25,588
- No, no, no, no.
- No, no?

972
01:23:25,839 --> 01:23:27,007
Tell us.

973
01:23:27,257 --> 01:23:30,719
A singing group all in one family.
You'll never guess, Georg.

974
01:23:30,969 --> 01:23:33,847
What a charming idea!

975
01:23:34,097 --> 01:23:36,516
Whose family?

976
01:23:36,766 --> 01:23:38,351
Yours.

977
01:23:38,601 --> 01:23:42,439
They'll be the talk of the festival.

978
01:23:43,773 --> 01:23:46,776
- Well, now, what's so funny?
- You are, Max.

979
01:23:47,027 --> 01:23:50,655
You're expensive, but very funny.

980
01:23:50,905 --> 01:23:53,241
- They'll be a sensation!
- No, Max.

981
01:23:53,491 --> 01:23:55,618
It's a wonderful idea.
Fresh, original.

982
01:23:55,869 --> 01:24:01,124
Max! My children
do not sing in public.

983
01:24:01,374 --> 01:24:03,293
You can't blame me for trying.

984
01:24:03,543 --> 01:24:08,089
Children, who shall we hear
from next?

985
01:24:09,341 --> 01:24:10,467
Who?

986
01:24:10,717 --> 01:24:13,303
Yes.

987
01:24:16,639 --> 01:24:19,434
Yes.

988
01:24:20,769 --> 01:24:23,271
The vote is unanimous.

989
01:24:23,521 --> 01:24:25,106
You, captain.

990
01:24:25,357 --> 01:24:27,150
Me?

991
01:24:27,400 --> 01:24:31,237
- I don't understand.
- Please.

992
01:24:35,909 --> 01:24:38,036
No, no, no, no.

993
01:24:38,286 --> 01:24:40,288
I'm told that you were quite good.

994
01:24:40,538 --> 01:24:43,875
- That was a very, very long time ago.
- I remember, Father.

995
01:24:44,125 --> 01:24:48,546
- Play us something we know.
- Oh, please, Father.

996
01:24:49,881 --> 01:24:52,509
Well...

997
01:25:01,226 --> 01:25:03,395
- Why didn't you tell me?
- What?

998
01:25:03,645 --> 01:25:07,524
To bring along my harmonica.

999
01:25:08,316 --> 01:25:11,986
{y:i}Edelweiss

1000
01:25:12,028 --> 01:25:15,156
{y:i}Edelweiss

1001
01:25:15,407 --> 01:25:21,621
{y:i}Every morning you greet me

1002
01:25:21,871 --> 01:25:25,500
{y:i}Small and white

1003
01:25:25,750 --> 01:25:29,087
{y:i}Clean and bright

1004
01:25:29,337 --> 01:25:32,132
{y:i}You look happy...

1005
01:25:32,382 --> 01:25:36,219
{y:i}... to meet me

1006
01:25:36,469 --> 01:25:38,763
{y:i}Blossom of snow

1007
01:25:39,014 --> 01:25:43,143
{y:i}May you bloom and grow

1008
01:25:43,393 --> 01:25:50,442
{y:i}Bloom and grow forever

1009
01:25:50,692 --> 01:25:54,320
{y:i}Edelweiss

1010
01:25:54,571 --> 01:25:57,615
{y:i}Edelweiss

1011
01:25:57,866 --> 01:26:06,666
{y:i}Bless my homeland forever

1012
01:26:09,544 --> 01:26:12,756
{y:i}- Edelweiss
{y:i}- Edelweiss

1013
01:26:13,006 --> 01:26:16,509
{y:i}- Edelweiss
{y:i}- Edelweiss

1014
01:26:16,760 --> 01:26:23,141
{y:i}Every morning you greet me

1015
01:26:23,391 --> 01:26:26,936
{y:i}- Small and white
{y:i}- Small and white

1016
01:26:27,187 --> 01:26:30,607
{y:i}- Clean and bright
{y:i}- Clean and bright

1017
01:26:30,857 --> 01:26:33,360
{y:i}You look happy...

1018
01:26:33,610 --> 01:26:37,447
{y:i}... to meet me

1019
01:26:37,697 --> 01:26:39,783
{y:i}Blossom of snow

1020
01:26:40,033 --> 01:26:44,162
{y:i}May you bloom and grow

1021
01:26:44,412 --> 01:26:51,461
{y:i}Bloom and grow forever

1022
01:26:51,711 --> 01:26:55,382
{y:i}Edelweiss

1023
01:26:55,632 --> 01:26:58,635
{y:i}Edelweiss

1024
01:26:58,885 --> 01:27:10,355
{y:i}Bless my homeland forever

1025
01:27:18,029 --> 01:27:21,700
Anytime you say the word, Georg,
you can be part of my new act:

1026
01:27:21,950 --> 01:27:25,286
The von Trapp Family Singers.

1027
01:27:25,537 --> 01:27:28,039
I have a wonderful idea, Georg.

1028
01:27:28,289 --> 01:27:30,750
Let's really fill this house
with music.

1029
01:27:31,001 --> 01:27:33,962
You must give a grand
and glorious party for me.

1030
01:27:34,212 --> 01:27:36,881
- A party?
- Yes, Father, please!

1031
01:27:37,132 --> 01:27:39,509
It's high time
I met all your friends...

1032
01:27:39,759 --> 01:27:42,303
...and they met me.
Don't you agree?

1033
01:27:42,554 --> 01:27:44,806
- I see what you mean.
- Oh, please!

1034
01:27:45,056 --> 01:27:48,893
Children, it's bedtime.
Come now, say good night.

1035
01:27:49,144 --> 01:27:50,437
Good night, Father.

1036
01:27:50,687 --> 01:27:53,481
- Good night.
- Good night, Baroness Schraeder.

1037
01:27:53,732 --> 01:27:56,484
Good night, Father.

1038
01:27:56,735 --> 01:27:58,028
Good night.

1039
01:27:58,278 --> 01:28:01,656
Good night, Uncle Max.

1040
01:28:02,032 --> 01:28:06,745
It'll be my first party, Father!

1041
01:28:37,734 --> 01:28:40,320
- Captain.
- Herr Zeller. Baroness Schraeder.

1042
01:28:40,570 --> 01:28:44,741
- Good evening, Herr Zeller.
- Baroness.

1043
01:28:44,991 --> 01:28:48,995
How do you do? Good evening.

1044
01:29:08,098 --> 01:29:13,061
Did you notice the obvious
display of the Austrian flag?

1045
01:29:18,608 --> 01:29:20,402
The women look so beautiful.

1046
01:29:20,652 --> 01:29:22,028
I think they look ugly.

1047
01:29:22,278 --> 01:29:24,030
You're just scared of them.

1048
01:29:24,280 --> 01:29:26,908
Silly, only grown-up men fear women.

1049
01:29:27,158 --> 01:29:32,288
- I think the men look beautiful.
- How would you know?

1050
01:29:47,971 --> 01:29:49,973
Liesl, who are you dancing with?

1051
01:29:50,223 --> 01:29:52,434
Nobody.

1052
01:29:52,684 --> 01:29:56,146
Oh, yes, you are.

1053
01:29:57,063 --> 01:29:58,523
May I have this dance?

1054
01:29:58,773 --> 01:30:02,819
I'd be delighted, young man.

1055
01:30:07,991 --> 01:30:10,702
Why didn't you tell me
you could dance?

1056
01:30:10,952 --> 01:30:17,417
We feared you'd make us all dance.
The von Trapp Family Dancers.

1057
01:30:20,295 --> 01:30:21,963
What are they playing?

1058
01:30:22,213 --> 01:30:24,758
It's the Laendler.
An Austrian folk dance.

1059
01:30:25,008 --> 01:30:28,178
- Show me.
- I haven't danced since I was little.

1060
01:30:28,428 --> 01:30:30,388
You remember. Please?

1061
01:30:30,638 --> 01:30:32,599
- Well...
- Please.

1062
01:30:32,849 --> 01:30:35,310
All right. Come on over here.

1063
01:30:35,560 --> 01:30:39,064
Now you bow and I curtsy.

1064
01:30:39,314 --> 01:30:42,275
- Like this?
- Fine. Now we go for a little walk.

1065
01:30:42,525 --> 01:30:45,195
One, two, three. One, two, three.

1066
01:30:45,445 --> 01:30:50,075
One, two, three, step together.
Now, step hop, step hop.

1067
01:30:50,325 --> 01:30:52,952
Now turn under. Not quite.

1068
01:30:53,203 --> 01:30:57,582
This way. Hop step, hop.
And under.

1069
01:30:57,832 --> 01:30:59,501
Kurt, we'll have to practice.

1070
01:30:59,751 --> 01:31:02,796
Do allow me, will you?

1071
01:32:29,549 --> 01:32:32,969
I don't remember anymore.

1072
01:32:33,595 --> 01:32:36,848
- Your face is all red.
- Is it?

1073
01:32:37,098 --> 01:32:39,184
I don't suppose I'm used to dancing.

1074
01:32:41,936 --> 01:32:43,897
Why, that was beautifully done.

1075
01:32:44,147 --> 01:32:46,066
What a lovely couple you make.

1076
01:32:46,316 --> 01:32:48,443
It's time the children
said good night.

1077
01:32:48,693 --> 01:32:50,028
We'll be in the hall.

1078
01:32:50,278 --> 01:32:53,156
- We have something special prepared.
- Right!

1079
01:32:53,406 --> 01:32:56,159
Yes, come on!

1080
01:33:01,456 --> 01:33:03,792
All that needless worrying, Georg.

1081
01:33:04,042 --> 01:33:08,630
You thought you wouldn't find
a friend at the party.

1082
01:33:10,006 --> 01:33:13,551
- A bit chilly out tonight, isn't it?
- Oh, I don't know.

1083
01:33:13,802 --> 01:33:17,347
It seemed rather warm to me.

1084
01:33:20,058 --> 01:33:21,726
Ladies and gentlemen.

1085
01:33:21,976 --> 01:33:27,607
The children of Captain von Trapp
wish to say good night to you.

1086
01:33:40,203 --> 01:33:44,040
{y:i}There's a sad sort of clanging
{y:i}From the clock in the hall

1087
01:33:44,290 --> 01:33:48,044
{y:i}And the bells in the steeple too

1088
01:33:48,294 --> 01:33:52,298
{y:i}And up in the nursery
{y:i}An absurd little bird

1089
01:33:52,549 --> 01:33:54,968
{y:i}Is popping up to say "coo-coo"

1090
01:33:55,218 --> 01:33:56,886
{y:i}Coo-coo

1091
01:33:57,137 --> 01:33:58,346
{y:i}Coo-coo

1092
01:33:58,596 --> 01:34:00,724
{y:i}- Regretfully they tell us
{y:i}- Coo-coo

1093
01:34:00,974 --> 01:34:02,350
{y:i}- But firmly they compel us
{y:i}- Coo-coo

1094
01:34:02,600 --> 01:34:05,645
{y:i}- To say goodbye
{y:i}- Coo-coo

1095
01:34:05,895 --> 01:34:12,736
{y:i}To you

1096
01:34:14,404 --> 01:34:18,241
{y:i}So long, farewell
{y:i}Auf Wiedersehen, good night

1097
01:34:18,491 --> 01:34:23,872
{y:i}I hate to go
{y:i}And leave this pretty sight

1098
01:34:29,586 --> 01:34:33,340
{y:i}So long, farewell,
{y:i}Auf Wiedersehen, adieu

1099
01:34:33,590 --> 01:34:38,678
{y:i}Adieu, adieu
{y:i}To you and you and you

1100
01:34:44,517 --> 01:34:48,313
{y:i}So long, farewell
{y:i}Au revoir, auf Wiedersehen

1101
01:34:48,563 --> 01:34:52,525
{y:i}I'd like to stay
{y:i}And taste my first champagne

1102
01:34:52,776 --> 01:34:56,738
- Yes?
- No.

1103
01:34:59,532 --> 01:35:03,286
{y:i}So long, farewell
{y:i}Auf Wiedersehen, goodbye

1104
01:35:03,536 --> 01:35:07,457
{y:i}I leave and heave a sigh
{y:i}And say goodbye

1105
01:35:07,707 --> 01:35:13,254
{y:i}Goodbye

1106
01:35:14,756 --> 01:35:17,842
{y:i}I'm glad to go

1107
01:35:18,093 --> 01:35:21,054
{y:i}I cannot tell a lie

1108
01:35:21,304 --> 01:35:24,307
{y:i}I flit, I float

1109
01:35:24,557 --> 01:35:29,562
{y:i}I fleetly flee, I fly

1110
01:35:38,071 --> 01:35:43,785
{y:i}The sun has gone to bed

1111
01:35:44,035 --> 01:35:47,706
{y:i}And so must I

1112
01:35:47,956 --> 01:35:50,375
{y:i}So long

1113
01:35:50,625 --> 01:35:53,128
{y:i}Farewell

1114
01:35:53,378 --> 01:35:58,800
{y:i}Auf Wiedersehen, goodbye

1115
01:35:59,050 --> 01:36:04,848
{y:i}Goodbye

1116
01:36:05,098 --> 01:36:10,645
{y:i}Goodbye

1117
01:36:10,895 --> 01:36:17,736
{y:i}Goodbye

1118
01:36:17,986 --> 01:36:27,996
{y:i}Goodbye

1119
01:36:31,499 --> 01:36:34,252
Extraordinary!
What they'd do at the festival.

1120
01:36:34,502 --> 01:36:37,714
Young lady,
I must have a word with you.

1121
01:36:37,964 --> 01:36:42,010
Georg, you won't let this girl
get away. She must join the party.

1122
01:36:42,260 --> 01:36:44,679
- No, really I...
- Stop. Stop it now.

1123
01:36:44,929 --> 01:36:47,849
- Georg, please.
- You can if you want to, fräulein.

1124
01:36:48,099 --> 01:36:50,560
I insist.
You will be my dinner partner.

1125
01:36:50,810 --> 01:36:52,729
This is business.
Franz...

1126
01:36:52,979 --> 01:36:57,692
...set another place next to mine
for Fräulein Maria.

1127
01:36:58,318 --> 01:37:01,571
- Whatever you say.
- It appears to be all arranged.

1128
01:37:01,821 --> 01:37:04,532
- It does.
- I'm not suitably dressed.

1129
01:37:04,783 --> 01:37:07,869
You can change. We'll wait.

1130
01:37:07,994 --> 01:37:09,662
All right.

1131
01:37:09,913 --> 01:37:12,791
Captain, you must be very proud
of your youngsters.

1132
01:37:13,041 --> 01:37:13,917
I am, thank you.

1133
01:37:14,167 --> 01:37:18,713
Is there a more beautiful expression
of what is good in our country...

1134
01:37:18,963 --> 01:37:22,550
...than the innocent voices
of our children?

1135
01:37:22,801 --> 01:37:24,177
Oh, come now, baron.

1136
01:37:24,427 --> 01:37:28,807
Would you have us believe that Austria
holds a monopoly on virtue?

1137
01:37:29,057 --> 01:37:33,186
Herr Zeller, some of us prefer
Austrian voices raised in song...

1138
01:37:33,436 --> 01:37:36,648
...to ugly German threats.

1139
01:37:36,898 --> 01:37:40,485
The ostrich buries his head
in the sand...

1140
01:37:40,735 --> 01:37:43,947
...and sometimes in the flag.

1141
01:37:44,197 --> 01:37:48,410
Perhaps those who would warn you that
the Anschluss is coming, and it is...

1142
01:37:48,660 --> 01:37:52,330
...would get further with you by setting
their words to music.

1143
01:37:52,580 --> 01:37:57,293
If the Nazis take over Austria, you
will be the entire trumpet section.

1144
01:37:57,544 --> 01:37:59,379
You flatter me.

1145
01:37:59,629 --> 01:38:05,051
Oh, how clumsy of me.
I meant to accuse you.

1146
01:38:11,599 --> 01:38:13,727
It's very kind of you
to offer to help me, baroness.

1147
01:38:13,977 --> 01:38:15,770
I'm delighted, Maria.

1148
01:38:16,021 --> 01:38:19,983
I really don't think I have anything
that would be appropriate.

1149
01:38:20,233 --> 01:38:24,362
Now where is that lovely little thing
you were wearing the other evening?

1150
01:38:24,612 --> 01:38:29,451
When the captain couldn't keep
his eyes off you.

1151
01:38:31,286 --> 01:38:34,164
Couldn't keep his eyes off me?

1152
01:38:34,414 --> 01:38:36,249
Come, my dear, we are women.

1153
01:38:36,499 --> 01:38:39,836
Let's not pretend we don't know
when a man notices us.

1154
01:38:40,086 --> 01:38:43,840
- Here we are.
- The captain notices everybody.

1155
01:38:44,090 --> 01:38:46,551
There's no need to feel
so defensive, Maria.

1156
01:38:46,801 --> 01:38:49,220
You are quite attractive, you know.

1157
01:38:49,471 --> 01:38:54,684
The captain would hardly be a man
if he didn't notice you.

1158
01:38:54,684 --> 01:38:57,312
Baroness, I hope you're joking.

1159
01:38:57,562 --> 01:38:58,813
Not at all.

1160
01:38:59,064 --> 01:39:00,648
I've never done a thing to...

1161
01:39:00,899 --> 01:39:02,901
You don't have to, my dear.

1162
01:39:03,151 --> 01:39:08,948
Nothing's more irresistible to a man
than a woman who's in love with him.

1163
01:39:09,199 --> 01:39:12,369
- In love with him?
- Of course.

1164
01:39:12,619 --> 01:39:17,582
What makes it so nice is
he thinks he's in love with you.

1165
01:39:18,750 --> 01:39:20,460
But that's not true.

1166
01:39:20,710 --> 01:39:24,255
Surely you've noticed the way
he looks into your eyes.

1167
01:39:24,506 --> 01:39:30,428
And you know, you blushed in his arms
when you were dancing just now.

1168
01:39:30,679 --> 01:39:32,389
Don't take it to heart.

1169
01:39:32,639 --> 01:39:34,933
He'll get over it soon enough,
I think.

1170
01:39:35,183 --> 01:39:37,936
Men do, you know.

1171
01:39:38,186 --> 01:39:40,313
Then I should go.

1172
01:39:40,563 --> 01:39:43,608
I mustn't stay here.

1173
01:39:52,534 --> 01:39:57,455
- Is there something I can do to help?
- No, nothing.

1174
01:39:57,747 --> 01:39:59,708
Yes.

1175
01:39:59,958 --> 01:40:03,545
Don't say a word to the captain.

1176
01:40:03,628 --> 01:40:08,717
No, I wouldn't dream of it.

1177
01:40:13,930 --> 01:40:16,099
Goodbye, Maria.

1178
01:40:16,349 --> 01:40:20,687
I'm sure you'll make a very fine nun.

1179
01:40:49,174 --> 01:40:51,343
Champagne, darling.

1180
01:40:51,593 --> 01:40:57,098
I feel like celebrating. Cheers.

1181
01:41:01,061 --> 01:41:03,938
- You know something.
- Perhaps.

1182
01:41:04,189 --> 01:41:08,735
If you're so clever, tell me how to
get fräulein to influence Georg.

1183
01:41:08,985 --> 01:41:11,780
I want those children in the festival.

1184
01:41:12,030 --> 01:41:14,324
Elsa, this is important to Austria.

1185
01:41:14,574 --> 01:41:16,284
Wouldn't do you any harm either.

1186
01:41:16,534 --> 01:41:18,078
I thought of that.

1187
01:41:18,328 --> 01:41:20,747
Well, if it's a matter of influence...

1188
01:41:20,997 --> 01:41:25,460
...maybe the one you have
to be talking to is me.

1189
01:44:50,457 --> 01:44:52,709
Two.
Three.

1190
01:44:52,959 --> 01:44:55,462
Four.
Five.

1191
01:44:55,712 --> 01:44:57,756
- Six.
- Seven.

1192
01:44:58,006 --> 01:44:59,632
Eight.

1193
01:44:59,883 --> 01:45:02,552
- Four.
- Two.

1194
01:45:02,802 --> 01:45:04,596
Seven.
Five.

1195
01:45:04,846 --> 01:45:08,600
- Two.
- Six.

1196
01:45:11,728 --> 01:45:14,856
Isn't this fun?

1197
01:45:15,106 --> 01:45:17,525
- Four.
- I'm number five.

1198
01:45:17,776 --> 01:45:19,944
- Oh, yes.
Eight.

1199
01:45:20,195 --> 01:45:22,739
Two.
Four.

1200
01:45:22,989 --> 01:45:25,700
Six.

1201
01:45:26,701 --> 01:45:28,828
Two.

1202
01:45:29,079 --> 01:45:32,040
Baroness Schraeder, do you mind
if we stop now? We're tired.

1203
01:45:32,290 --> 01:45:34,250
Whatever you want, dear.

1204
01:45:34,501 --> 01:45:38,004
We'll do it again tomorrow.

1205
01:45:46,680 --> 01:45:49,641
The country's so restful, isn't it?

1206
01:45:49,891 --> 01:45:52,519
Have some lemonade.

1207
01:45:52,769 --> 01:45:55,313
There must be an easier way.

1208
01:45:55,563 --> 01:45:59,567
I get a fiendish delight thinking
of you as the mother of seven.

1209
01:45:59,818 --> 01:46:01,653
How do you plan to do it?

1210
01:46:01,903 --> 01:46:03,947
Darling, haven't you ever heard...

1211
01:46:04,197 --> 01:46:08,159
...of a delightful little thing
called boarding school?

1212
01:46:08,410 --> 01:46:11,788
Baroness Machiavelli.

1213
01:46:12,038 --> 01:46:14,040
Uncle Max, where's Father?

1214
01:46:14,290 --> 01:46:16,084
I think he's in the house.

1215
01:46:16,334 --> 01:46:18,878
What's the matter
with all you gloomy pussies?

1216
01:46:19,129 --> 01:46:21,881
- Nothing.
- I know. Let's have a rehearsal.

1217
01:46:22,132 --> 01:46:22,966
What for?

1218
01:46:23,216 --> 01:46:25,677
Let's make believe we're on-stage
at the festival.

1219
01:46:25,927 --> 01:46:29,806
- I don't feel like singing.
- Not without Fräulein Maria.

1220
01:46:30,056 --> 01:46:32,434
Liesl, get the guitar.
Come on, Marta.

1221
01:46:32,684 --> 01:46:35,645
Everybody into the group.
Get in your places.

1222
01:46:35,895 --> 01:46:40,191
Now be cheerful, right?
Give us the key, Liesl.

1223
01:46:40,567 --> 01:46:43,194
Now, impress me.

1224
01:46:47,240 --> 01:46:48,616
Gretl, why don't you sing?

1225
01:46:48,867 --> 01:46:52,120
I can't. I've got a sore finger.

1226
01:46:52,370 --> 01:46:55,373
But you sang so beautifully
the night of the party.

1227
01:46:55,623 --> 01:46:57,959
Come on, all of you.
Try something you know.

1228
01:46:58,209 --> 01:46:59,711
Enjoy it. Be cheerful.

1229
01:46:59,961 --> 01:47:03,131
All right, Liesl.

1230
01:47:03,548 --> 01:47:08,928
{y:i}The hills are alive

1231
01:47:09,179 --> 01:47:17,812
{y:i}With the sound of music

1232
01:47:18,063 --> 01:47:23,109
{y:i}With songs they have sung

1233
01:47:23,360 --> 01:47:31,618
{y:i}For a thousand years

1234
01:47:31,868 --> 01:47:37,290
{y:i}The hills fill my heart

1235
01:47:37,540 --> 01:47:45,340
{y:i}With the sound of music

1236
01:47:45,590 --> 01:47:48,218
They wanted to sing for me,
bless their hearts.

1237
01:47:48,468 --> 01:47:51,888
That's lovely, lovely.
Don't stop.

1238
01:47:54,391 --> 01:47:58,478
- Something long and cool, Georg?
- No, thank you, darling.

1239
01:47:58,728 --> 01:48:01,356
- Father?
- Yes, Brigitta?

1240
01:48:01,606 --> 01:48:03,900
Is it true Fräulein Maria
isn't coming back?

1241
01:48:04,150 --> 01:48:07,320
Yes, I suppose it's true.
What have we got here?

1242
01:48:07,570 --> 01:48:12,742
Pink lemonade.
Laced with lemonade.

1243
01:48:12,992 --> 01:48:14,536
I don't believe it, Father.

1244
01:48:14,786 --> 01:48:15,662
Hm?

1245
01:48:15,912 --> 01:48:18,081
- What?
- About Fräulein Maria.

1246
01:48:18,331 --> 01:48:19,582
Oh, Fräulein Maria!

1247
01:48:19,833 --> 01:48:23,795
Didn't I tell you what her note said?
I'm sure I did.

1248
01:48:24,045 --> 01:48:26,589
She said she missed her life
at the abbey.

1249
01:48:26,840 --> 01:48:28,967
She had to leave us.

1250
01:48:29,217 --> 01:48:32,387
And that's all there is to it.

1251
01:48:32,554 --> 01:48:35,181
I think I'm brave enough to try
some of that.

1252
01:48:35,432 --> 01:48:38,184
- She didn't even say goodbye.
- She did in her note.

1253
01:48:38,435 --> 01:48:40,270
That isn't the same thing.

1254
01:48:40,520 --> 01:48:42,689
Not too sweet, not too sour.

1255
01:48:42,939 --> 01:48:46,109
Just too pink.

1256
01:48:46,359 --> 01:48:50,989
Father, who is our new governess
going to be?

1257
01:48:56,119 --> 01:48:58,663
Well...

1258
01:48:59,497 --> 01:49:04,878
...you're not going to have
a governess anymore.

1259
01:49:05,128 --> 01:49:09,257
- We're not?
- No.

1260
01:49:10,008 --> 01:49:12,969
You're going to have a new mother.

1261
01:49:13,219 --> 01:49:15,805
A new mother?

1262
01:49:16,056 --> 01:49:19,184
We talked about it last night.
It's all settled.

1263
01:49:19,434 --> 01:49:23,646
And we're all going to be very happy.

1264
01:49:55,136 --> 01:49:59,099
Well, all right, all right.
Run off and play.

1265
01:50:29,587 --> 01:50:30,505
Yes, my children?

1266
01:50:30,755 --> 01:50:33,383
- My name is Liesl.
- Yes, Liesl?

1267
01:50:33,633 --> 01:50:38,513
We, my brothers and sisters,
want to see Fräulein Maria.

1268
01:50:38,763 --> 01:50:41,224
Fräulein Maria?

1269
01:50:41,474 --> 01:50:44,978
Oh, Maria.

1270
01:50:45,395 --> 01:50:48,732
Come in, please.

1271
01:50:51,151 --> 01:50:54,154
Wait here.

1272
01:51:03,580 --> 01:51:06,791
I'm Sister Margaretta. I understand
you inquired about Maria.

1273
01:51:07,042 --> 01:51:09,544
We have to see her.
Will you tell her we're here?

1274
01:51:09,794 --> 01:51:12,380
- I'm afraid I can't do that.
- But you've got to!

1275
01:51:12,630 --> 01:51:14,674
- She's our governess.
- We want her back.

1276
01:51:14,924 --> 01:51:16,843
She didn't even say goodbye.

1277
01:51:17,093 --> 01:51:19,387
All we want to do is talk to her.

1278
01:51:19,637 --> 01:51:22,515
I'm very sorry,
but Maria is in seclusion.

1279
01:51:22,766 --> 01:51:25,852
- She hasn't been seeing anyone.
- She'll see us.

1280
01:51:26,102 --> 01:51:28,563
I want to show her my finger.

1281
01:51:28,813 --> 01:51:30,732
Some other time, dear.

1282
01:51:30,982 --> 01:51:32,275
I'll tell her you were here.

1283
01:51:32,525 --> 01:51:35,987
- It was sweet of you to call.
- We have to speak to her!

1284
01:51:36,237 --> 01:51:39,324
Run along, children.
Run along home.

1285
01:51:39,574 --> 01:51:41,242
I'm sure she'd like to see us.

1286
01:51:41,493 --> 01:51:43,703
Sister Margaretta, please.

1287
01:51:43,953 --> 01:51:49,209
- Goodbye, children.
Sister Margaretta, please?

1288
01:51:50,502 --> 01:51:52,212
What was that about, Sister?

1289
01:51:52,462 --> 01:51:54,631
The von Trapp children,
Reverend Mother.

1290
01:51:54,881 --> 01:51:57,342
They want to see Maria.

1291
01:51:57,592 --> 01:51:59,928
Has she spoken yet?
Has she told you anything?

1292
01:52:00,178 --> 01:52:04,182
She doesn't say a word,
Reverend Mother, except in prayer.

1293
01:52:04,432 --> 01:52:05,392
Poor child.

1294
01:52:05,642 --> 01:52:08,603
It's strange.
She seems happy to be back here...

1295
01:52:08,853 --> 01:52:13,108
...and yet she's unhappy too.

1296
01:52:13,358 --> 01:52:16,486
Perhaps I have been wrong
in leaving her alone so long.

1297
01:52:16,736 --> 01:52:19,364
Bring her to me,
even if she's not yet ready.

1298
01:52:19,614 --> 01:52:22,784
Yes, Reverend Mother.

1299
01:52:26,788 --> 01:52:31,084
Sister Augusta, take our new postulant
to the robing room.

1300
01:52:31,334 --> 01:52:33,545
God bless you, my daughter.

1301
01:52:39,801 --> 01:52:43,054
Yes, bring her in.

1302
01:52:50,562 --> 01:52:54,274
You've been unhappy. I'm sorry.

1303
01:52:54,816 --> 01:52:56,151
Reverend Mother.

1304
01:52:56,401 --> 01:52:59,612
Why did they send you back to us?

1305
01:52:59,863 --> 01:53:03,324
They didn't send me back.
I left.

1306
01:53:04,325 --> 01:53:06,161
Sit down, Maria.

1307
01:53:06,411 --> 01:53:09,748
Tell me what happened.

1308
01:53:13,418 --> 01:53:14,711
I was frightened.

1309
01:53:14,961 --> 01:53:19,466
- Frightened? Were they unkind to you.
- Oh, no!

1310
01:53:19,716 --> 01:53:24,637
No, I was confused. I felt...

1311
01:53:24,888 --> 01:53:26,973
I've never felt that way before.

1312
01:53:27,223 --> 01:53:30,977
I couldn't stay. I knew that here
I'd be away from it. I'd be safe.

1313
01:53:31,227 --> 01:53:35,940
Maria, our abbey is not to be used
as an escape.

1314
01:53:36,191 --> 01:53:39,944
What is it you can't face?

1315
01:53:40,403 --> 01:53:42,405
I can't face him again.

1316
01:53:42,655 --> 01:53:45,658
Him?

1317
01:53:45,784 --> 01:53:49,329
Thank you, Sister Margaretta.

1318
01:53:57,420 --> 01:54:00,590
Captain von Trapp?

1319
01:54:04,511 --> 01:54:07,222
- Are you in love with him?
- I don't know!

1320
01:54:07,472 --> 01:54:09,224
I don't know. I...

1321
01:54:09,474 --> 01:54:13,311
The baroness said I was.
She said that he was in love with me.

1322
01:54:13,561 --> 01:54:17,065
But I didn't want to believe it.

1323
01:54:17,315 --> 01:54:19,567
There were times we looked
at each other.

1324
01:54:19,818 --> 01:54:21,444
I could hardly breathe.

1325
01:54:21,695 --> 01:54:24,781
- Did you let him see your feelings?
- I don't know.

1326
01:54:25,031 --> 01:54:27,659
That's what's torturing me.
I was on God's errand.

1327
01:54:27,909 --> 01:54:34,374
To have asked for his love would have
been wrong. I just couldn't stay.

1328
01:54:35,041 --> 01:54:38,503
I'm ready at this moment
to take my vows.

1329
01:54:38,753 --> 01:54:43,383
- Please help me.
- Maria.

1330
01:54:43,383 --> 01:54:48,096
The love of a man and a woman is holy.
You have a great capacity to love.

1331
01:54:48,346 --> 01:54:51,266
You must find out how God
wants you to spend your love.

1332
01:54:51,516 --> 01:54:55,353
But I pledged my life to God.
I pledged my life to his service.

1333
01:54:55,603 --> 01:55:01,526
My daughter, if you love this man,
it doesn't mean you love God less.

1334
01:55:01,651 --> 01:55:03,486
No.

1335
01:55:03,737 --> 01:55:07,115
You must find out.

1336
01:55:07,365 --> 01:55:09,868
You must go back.

1337
01:55:10,118 --> 01:55:12,037
You can't ask me to do that.

1338
01:55:12,287 --> 01:55:14,748
- Please let me stay. I beg...
- Maria.

1339
01:55:14,998 --> 01:55:18,918
These walls were not built
to shut out problems.

1340
01:55:19,169 --> 01:55:21,087
You have to face them.

1341
01:55:21,338 --> 01:55:26,134
You have to live the life
you were born to live.

1342
01:55:30,430 --> 01:55:34,768
{y:i}Climb every mountain

1343
01:55:35,018 --> 01:55:38,772
{y:i}Search high and low

1344
01:55:39,022 --> 01:55:43,777
{y:i}Follow every byway

1345
01:55:44,027 --> 01:55:48,490
{y:i}Every path you know

1346
01:55:48,740 --> 01:55:53,203
{y:i}Climb every mountain

1347
01:55:53,453 --> 01:55:57,791
{y:i}Ford every stream

1348
01:55:58,041 --> 01:56:02,420
{y:i}Follow every rainbow

1349
01:56:02,671 --> 01:56:06,966
{y:i}Till you find your dream

1350
01:56:07,217 --> 01:56:10,303
{y:i}A dream that will need

1351
01:56:10,553 --> 01:56:15,892
{y:i}All the love you can give

1352
01:56:16,142 --> 01:56:19,562
{y:i}Every day of your life

1353
01:56:19,813 --> 01:56:26,277
{y:i}For as long as you live

1354
01:56:26,528 --> 01:56:30,824
{y:i}Climb every mountain

1355
01:56:31,074 --> 01:56:35,412
{y:i}Ford every stream

1356
01:56:35,662 --> 01:56:40,208
{y:i}Follow every rainbow

1357
01:56:40,458 --> 01:56:49,718
{y:i}Till you find your dream

1358
01:56:49,968 --> 01:56:53,471
{y:i}A dream that will need

1359
01:56:53,722 --> 01:56:59,310
{y:i}All the love you can give

1360
01:56:59,561 --> 01:57:03,023
{y:i}Every day of your life

1361
01:57:03,273 --> 01:57:09,738
{y:i}For as long as you live

1362
01:57:09,988 --> 01:57:14,409
{y:i}Climb every mountain

1363
01:57:14,659 --> 01:57:18,997
{y:i}Ford every stream

1364
01:57:19,247 --> 01:57:24,044
{y:i}Follow every rainbow

1365
01:57:24,294 --> 01:57:28,381
{y:i}Till you find...

1366
01:57:28,631 --> 01:57:40,101
{y:i}... your dream

1367
01:57:41,186 --> 01:57:44,689
Now, it's not like my children
to be secretive.

1368
01:57:44,939 --> 01:57:48,526
We're not being secretive, Father.

1369
01:57:48,735 --> 01:57:52,072
And it's not like my children
to be late for dinner.

1370
01:57:52,322 --> 01:57:56,326
- We lost track of the time.
- I see.

1371
01:57:56,576 --> 01:57:59,996
Who's going to be the first one
to tell me the truth? Friedrich.

1372
01:58:00,246 --> 01:58:03,416
Brigitta. Liesl.

1373
01:58:03,667 --> 01:58:06,670
Where do you think we were, Father?

1374
01:58:06,920 --> 01:58:09,589
No?

1375
01:58:12,509 --> 01:58:17,972
If you don't believe us, you must have
some idea of where you think we were.

1376
01:58:20,058 --> 01:58:21,810
- Marta.
- Yes, Father.

1377
01:58:22,060 --> 01:58:24,354
You tell me.

1378
01:58:24,604 --> 01:58:28,858
Friedrich told you, Father.
We were berry picking.

1379
01:58:29,109 --> 01:58:33,738
- I forgot! You were berry picking.
- Yes, we love berry picking.

1380
01:58:33,988 --> 01:58:35,115
All afternoon?

1381
01:58:35,365 --> 01:58:37,033
- We picked thousands.
- Thousands?

1382
01:58:37,283 --> 01:58:40,203
- They were all over the place.
- What kind of berries?

1383
01:58:40,453 --> 01:58:45,250
- Blueberries, sir.
- Blueberries.

1384
01:58:45,667 --> 01:58:50,255
It's too early for blueberries.

1385
01:58:50,505 --> 01:58:53,091
- They were strawberries.
- Strawberries?

1386
01:58:53,341 --> 01:58:57,554
It's been so cold lately,
they turned blue.

1387
01:58:58,763 --> 01:59:01,599
Aw.

1388
01:59:01,850 --> 01:59:05,020
Very well. Show me the berries.

1389
01:59:05,270 --> 01:59:06,521
- We...
- Well...

1390
01:59:06,771 --> 01:59:09,524
- Show me the berries you picked.
- We don't have them.

1391
01:59:09,774 --> 01:59:12,027
You don't have them?
What happened to them?

1392
01:59:12,277 --> 01:59:15,030
- We...
- We ate them.

1393
01:59:15,280 --> 01:59:17,824
- You ate them?
- Yes!

1394
01:59:18,074 --> 01:59:19,617
- They were so good.
- Delicious.

1395
01:59:19,868 --> 01:59:21,119
Very well.

1396
01:59:21,369 --> 01:59:26,499
Since you've obviously stuffed
yourselves on thousands of berries...

1397
01:59:26,750 --> 01:59:30,670
...you can't be hungry anymore,
so I'll have to tell Frau Schmidt...

1398
01:59:30,920 --> 01:59:35,300
...to skip your dinner.

1399
01:59:42,974 --> 01:59:45,727
It's your fault.
We should have told him the truth.

1400
01:59:45,977 --> 01:59:47,937
And made him boiling mad at us?

1401
01:59:48,188 --> 01:59:50,106
It's better than starving to death.

1402
01:59:50,357 --> 01:59:54,277
We didn't do anything wrong.
We just wanted to see her.

1403
01:59:54,527 --> 01:59:57,822
My stomach's making noises.

1404
01:59:58,073 --> 02:00:01,785
The least they could have done
was to let us say hello.

1405
02:00:02,035 --> 02:00:06,122
- I wonder what grass tastes like.
- I feel awful.

1406
02:00:06,373 --> 02:00:11,002
When Fräulein Maria wanted to feel
better she used to sing that song.

1407
02:00:11,252 --> 02:00:12,712
Let's try it.

1408
02:00:12,962 --> 02:00:15,632
{y:i}Raindrops on roses

1409
02:00:15,882 --> 02:00:20,553
{y:i}And whiskers on kittens

1410
02:00:20,804 --> 02:00:23,348
{y:i}Bright copper kettles

1411
02:00:23,598 --> 02:00:27,769
{y:i}And warm woolen mittens

1412
02:00:28,019 --> 02:00:33,983
{y:i}Brown paper packages
{y:i}Tied up with strings

1413
02:00:34,234 --> 02:00:40,240
{y:i}These are a few of my favorite things

1414
02:00:40,490 --> 02:00:43,451
Why don't I feel better?

1415
02:00:43,702 --> 02:00:45,995
{y:i}Girls in white dresses

1416
02:00:46,246 --> 02:00:49,332
{y:i}With blue satin sashes

1417
02:00:49,582 --> 02:00:55,380
{y:i}Snowflakes that stay
{y:i}On my nose and eyelashes

1418
02:00:55,630 --> 02:00:58,717
{y:i}- Silver white winters...
Fräulein Maria's back!

1419
02:00:58,967 --> 02:01:00,468
{y:i}... that melt into springs

1420
02:01:00,719 --> 02:01:03,930
{y:i}These are a few of my favorite things

1421
02:01:04,180 --> 02:01:07,267
{y:i}When the dog bites
{y:i}When the bee stings

1422
02:01:07,517 --> 02:01:09,894
{y:i}When I'm feeling sad

1423
02:01:10,145 --> 02:01:13,106
{y:i}I simply remember my favorite things

1424
02:01:13,356 --> 02:01:15,608
{y:i}And then I don't feel

1425
02:01:15,859 --> 02:01:22,574
{y:i}So bad

1426
02:01:22,824 --> 02:01:25,827
- Children, I'm so glad to see you.
- We missed you.

1427
02:01:26,077 --> 02:01:27,162
I missed you.

1428
02:01:27,412 --> 02:01:31,041
- Kurt, how are you?
- Hungry.

1429
02:01:31,082 --> 02:01:33,710
- What happened to your finger?
- It got caught.

1430
02:01:33,960 --> 02:01:37,088
- Caught in what?
- Friedrich's teeth.

1431
02:01:37,339 --> 02:01:39,507
- Liesl, you all right?
- Just fair.

1432
02:01:39,758 --> 02:01:42,719
- Any telegrams been delivered lately?
- None at all.

1433
02:01:42,969 --> 02:01:46,431
But I'm learning to accept it.
I'll be glad when school begins.

1434
02:01:46,681 --> 02:01:51,394
Liesl, you can't use school to escape
your problems. You have to face them.

1435
02:01:51,644 --> 02:01:53,104
I have so much to tell you.

1436
02:01:53,355 --> 02:01:55,440
We have things to tell you too.

1437
02:01:55,690 --> 02:01:59,361
The most important thing is
that Father is going to be married.

1438
02:01:59,611 --> 02:02:00,737
Married?

1439
02:02:00,987 --> 02:02:04,532
Yes, to Baroness Schraeder.

1440
02:02:10,914 --> 02:02:14,000
Oh, I see.

1441
02:02:14,334 --> 02:02:17,671
Father, look!
Fräulein Maria's back!

1442
02:02:17,921 --> 02:02:21,466
Fräulein Maria's back from the abbey.

1443
02:02:22,175 --> 02:02:24,761
Good evening, captain.

1444
02:02:24,761 --> 02:02:27,555
Good evening.

1445
02:02:28,556 --> 02:02:30,433
Everyone inside for dinner.

1446
02:02:30,684 --> 02:02:33,853
Dinner!

1447
02:02:41,653 --> 02:02:47,075
You left without saying goodbye.
Even to the children.

1448
02:02:47,325 --> 02:02:50,412
It was wrong of me. Forgive me.

1449
02:02:50,954 --> 02:02:52,080
Why did you?

1450
02:02:52,330 --> 02:02:56,084
Please don't ask me.
The reason no longer exists.

1451
02:02:56,334 --> 02:03:00,380
Fräulein Maria, you've returned.

1452
02:03:00,630 --> 02:03:03,967
Isn't it wonderful, Georg?

1453
02:03:05,760 --> 02:03:07,929
I wish you every happiness,
baroness.

1454
02:03:08,179 --> 02:03:10,932
You too, captain.
The children say you're to marry.

1455
02:03:11,182 --> 02:03:13,768
Thank you, my dear.

1456
02:03:14,853 --> 02:03:19,899
You are back to stay?

1457
02:03:20,483 --> 02:03:25,363
Only until arrangements can be made
for another governess.

1458
02:04:19,501 --> 02:04:22,671
There you are.

1459
02:04:35,475 --> 02:04:38,019
I must speak to cook
about the schnitzel.

1460
02:04:38,269 --> 02:04:41,731
It is entirely too delicious
for my figure.

1461
02:04:41,981 --> 02:04:44,943
And it makes you much too quiet
at the dinner table.

1462
02:04:45,193 --> 02:04:47,195
Or was it the wine?

1463
02:04:47,445 --> 02:04:50,782
Undoubtedly the wine.

1464
02:04:51,825 --> 02:04:54,119
You have no idea
the trouble I'm having...

1465
02:04:54,369 --> 02:04:57,122
...trying to decide on
a wedding present for you.

1466
02:04:57,372 --> 02:04:59,541
Oh, I know. I'm enough.

1467
02:04:59,791 --> 02:05:03,420
But I do want you to have
some little trifle for the occasion.

1468
02:05:03,670 --> 02:05:06,006
At first I thought
of a fountain pen...

1469
02:05:06,256 --> 02:05:08,508
...but you've already got one.

1470
02:05:08,758 --> 02:05:11,720
Then I thought perhaps a villa
in the south of France...

1471
02:05:11,970 --> 02:05:14,639
...but they are so difficult
to gift-wrap.

1472
02:05:14,889 --> 02:05:17,308
Oh, Georg, how do you
feel about yachts?

1473
02:05:17,559 --> 02:05:19,853
A long, sleek one
for the Mediterranean...

1474
02:05:20,103 --> 02:05:24,399
...or a tiny one
for your bathtub, huh?

1475
02:05:25,025 --> 02:05:27,068
- Elsa.
- Where to go on our honeymoon?

1476
02:05:27,318 --> 02:05:28,862
Now, that's a real problem.

1477
02:05:29,112 --> 02:05:32,490
A trip around the world
would be lovely. And then I said:

1478
02:05:32,741 --> 02:05:35,618
"Oh, Elsa, there must be
someplace better to go".

1479
02:05:35,869 --> 02:05:38,747
- But don't worry, darling, I'll...
- Elsa.

1480
02:05:38,997 --> 02:05:40,999
Yes, Georg.

1481
02:05:41,249 --> 02:05:43,543
It's no use...

1482
02:05:43,793 --> 02:05:46,713
...you and I.

1483
02:05:46,713 --> 02:05:51,051
I'm being dishonest to both of us...

1484
02:05:51,301 --> 02:05:54,471
...and utterly unfair to you.

1485
02:05:58,183 --> 02:06:01,603
- When two people talk of marriage...
- No, don't.

1486
02:06:01,853 --> 02:06:05,440
Don't say another word, please.

1487
02:06:07,233 --> 02:06:12,947
You see, there are other things
I've been thinking of.

1488
02:06:13,198 --> 02:06:19,412
Fond as I am of you, I really don't
think you're the right man for me.

1489
02:06:20,455 --> 02:06:22,957
You're much too independent.

1490
02:06:23,208 --> 02:06:27,962
And I need someone
who needs me desperately...

1491
02:06:28,213 --> 02:06:32,759
...or at least needs
my money desperately.

1492
02:06:33,426 --> 02:06:39,849
I've enjoyed every moment we've had
together and I do thank you for that.

1493
02:06:41,101 --> 02:06:42,977
Now, if you'll forgive me...

1494
02:06:43,228 --> 02:06:45,271
...I'll go inside,
pack my little bags...

1495
02:06:45,522 --> 02:06:49,526
...and return to Vienna
where I belong.

1496
02:06:53,405 --> 02:06:55,240
And somewhere out there...

1497
02:06:55,490 --> 02:06:59,911
...is a young lady who, I think...

1498
02:07:00,161 --> 02:07:02,914
...will never be a nun.

1499
02:07:13,550 --> 02:07:17,387
Auf Wiedersehen, darling.

1500
02:07:41,619 --> 02:07:44,289
Hello.

1501
02:07:48,585 --> 02:07:53,673
I thought I just might find you here.

1502
02:07:56,343 --> 02:07:58,762
Was there something you wanted?

1503
02:07:59,012 --> 02:08:02,849
No, no, no. Sit down, please.

1504
02:08:03,099 --> 02:08:06,019
Please.

1505
02:08:09,564 --> 02:08:12,484
May I?

1506
02:08:23,328 --> 02:08:28,917
You know, I was thinking
and I was wondering two things:

1507
02:08:29,167 --> 02:08:34,005
Why did you run away to the abbey?

1508
02:08:34,255 --> 02:08:40,470
And what was it
that made you come back?

1509
02:08:40,637 --> 02:08:45,058
Well, I had an obligation
to fulfill...

1510
02:08:45,308 --> 02:08:47,811
...and I came back to fulfill it.

1511
02:08:48,061 --> 02:08:50,605
Is that all?

1512
02:08:50,855 --> 02:08:53,066
And I missed the children.

1513
02:08:53,316 --> 02:08:55,527
Yes.

1514
02:08:55,777 --> 02:08:58,655
- Only the children?
- No. Yes.

1515
02:08:58,905 --> 02:09:02,701
- Isn't it right that I missed them?
- Oh, yes. Yes, of course.

1516
02:09:02,951 --> 02:09:06,454
I was only hoping that perhaps you...

1517
02:09:06,705 --> 02:09:09,958
Perhaps you might...

1518
02:09:10,208 --> 02:09:12,919
Yes?

1519
02:09:13,169 --> 02:09:18,967
Well, nothing was the same
when you were away...

1520
02:09:19,217 --> 02:09:22,470
...and it'll be all wrong again
after you leave...

1521
02:09:22,721 --> 02:09:28,893
...and I just thought perhaps
you might change your mind.

1522
02:09:31,438 --> 02:09:35,650
Well, I'm sure the baroness will be
able to make things fine for you.

1523
02:09:35,900 --> 02:09:38,820
Maria...

1524
02:09:39,404 --> 02:09:44,409
- There isn't going to be any baroness.
- There isn't?

1525
02:09:45,285 --> 02:09:46,703
No.

1526
02:09:46,953 --> 02:09:50,290
I don't understand.

1527
02:09:51,124 --> 02:09:55,253
Well, we've called off our engagement,
you see, and...

1528
02:09:55,503 --> 02:09:59,841
- Oh, I'm sorry.
- Yes. You are?

1529
02:10:00,091 --> 02:10:03,136
- You did?
- Yes.

1530
02:10:03,386 --> 02:10:09,434
Well, you can't marry someone
when you're...

1531
02:10:10,435 --> 02:10:14,022
...in love with someone else...

1532
02:10:15,940 --> 02:10:18,860
...can you?

1533
02:10:52,227 --> 02:10:54,521
The Reverend Mother always says:

1534
02:10:54,771 --> 02:10:59,693
"When the Lord closes a door,
somewhere He opens a window".

1535
02:11:06,574 --> 02:11:10,328
What else does
the Reverend Mother say?

1536
02:11:10,578 --> 02:11:14,499
That you have to look for your life.

1537
02:11:14,666 --> 02:11:18,670
Is that why you came back?

1538
02:11:21,214 --> 02:11:23,466
And have you found it...

1539
02:11:23,717 --> 02:11:26,386
...Maria?

1540
02:11:26,636 --> 02:11:30,140
I think I have.

1541
02:11:31,391 --> 02:11:34,310
I know I have.

1542
02:11:35,311 --> 02:11:38,481
I love you.

1543
02:11:40,942 --> 02:11:44,946
Oh, can this be happening to me?

1544
02:11:46,197 --> 02:11:53,079
{y:i}Perhaps I had a wicked childhood

1545
02:11:53,079 --> 02:11:59,836
{y:i}Perhaps I had a miserable youth

1546
02:12:00,211 --> 02:12:06,384
{y:i}But somewhere
{y:i}In my wicked, miserable past

1547
02:12:06,634 --> 02:12:13,099
{y:i}There must have been a moment of truth

1548
02:12:13,350 --> 02:12:20,607
{y:i}For here you are
{y:i}Standing there loving me

1549
02:12:20,857 --> 02:12:27,280
{y:i}Whether or not you should

1550
02:12:27,530 --> 02:12:34,913
{y:i}So somewhere in my youth or childhood

1551
02:12:35,163 --> 02:12:43,838
{y:i}I must have done something good

1552
02:12:44,089 --> 02:12:47,926
{y:i}Nothing comes from nothing

1553
02:12:48,176 --> 02:12:52,639
{y:i}Nothing ever could

1554
02:12:52,889 --> 02:13:03,274
{y:i}So somewhere in my youth or childhood

1555
02:13:03,274 --> 02:13:13,451
{y:i}I must have done something good

1556
02:13:13,702 --> 02:13:18,707
Do you know when I first
started loving you?

1557
02:13:18,957 --> 02:13:22,836
That night at dinner, when you sat
on that ridiculous pine cone.

1558
02:13:23,086 --> 02:13:25,463
What?

1559
02:13:25,714 --> 02:13:29,843
I knew the first time you blew
that silly whistle.

1560
02:13:30,093 --> 02:13:33,596
Oh, my love.

1561
02:13:35,849 --> 02:13:45,275
{y:i}For here you are
{y:i}Standing there loving me

1562
02:13:45,734 --> 02:13:52,699
{y:i}Whether or not you should

1563
02:13:52,949 --> 02:14:00,498
{y:i}So somewhere in my youth or childhood

1564
02:14:00,749 --> 02:14:09,424
{y:i}I must have done something good

1565
02:14:09,674 --> 02:14:13,636
{y:i}Nothing comes from nothing

1566
02:14:13,887 --> 02:14:16,931
{y:i}Nothing ever could

1567
02:14:17,182 --> 02:14:22,979
{y:i}So somewhere in my youth...

1568
02:14:23,229 --> 02:14:26,983
{y:i}... or childhood

1569
02:14:28,568 --> 02:14:36,618
{y:i}I must have done something

1570
02:14:37,160 --> 02:14:51,299
{y:i}Something good

1571
02:14:56,513 --> 02:14:59,516
Maria.

1572
02:14:59,891 --> 02:15:05,980
Is there anyone I should go to,
to ask permission to marry you?

1573
02:15:08,400 --> 02:15:11,861
- Why don't we ask...
- The children?

1574
02:16:59,469 --> 02:17:04,015
{y:i}How do you solve a problem like Maria?

1575
02:17:04,265 --> 02:17:08,728
{y:i}How do you catch a cloud
{y:i}And pin it down?

1576
02:17:08,978 --> 02:17:13,274
{y:i}How do you find a word
{y:i}That means Maria?

1577
02:17:13,525 --> 02:17:14,526
{y:i}A flibbertigibbet

1578
02:17:14,776 --> 02:17:18,029
{y:i}A will-o'-the-wisp
{y:i}A clown

1579
02:17:18,279 --> 02:17:22,992
{y:i}Many a thing you know
{y:i}You'd like to tell her

1580
02:17:23,243 --> 02:17:27,789
{y:i}Many a thing she ought to understand

1581
02:17:28,039 --> 02:17:32,502
{y:i}But how do you make her stay
{y:i}And listen to all you say?

1582
02:17:32,752 --> 02:17:36,715
{y:i}How do you keep a wave upon the sand?

1583
02:17:36,965 --> 02:17:42,095
{y:i}Oh, how do you solve
{y:i}A problem like Maria?

1584
02:17:42,345 --> 02:17:50,478
{y:i}How do you hold a moonbeam
{y:i}In your hand?

1585
02:19:55,061 --> 02:19:58,148
Herr Detweiler!

1586
02:19:59,232 --> 02:20:01,901
- Heil Hitler.
- Oh, good afternoon, Herr Zeller.

1587
02:20:02,152 --> 02:20:05,447
Perhaps you've not heard.
I am now the Gauleiter.

1588
02:20:05,697 --> 02:20:07,949
Heil Hitler.

1589
02:20:08,199 --> 02:20:09,367
Heil Hitler.

1590
02:20:09,617 --> 02:20:12,120
I've come from
Captain von Trapp's house.

1591
02:20:12,370 --> 02:20:15,957
The only one in the area
not flying the Third Reich flag...

1592
02:20:16,207 --> 02:20:17,417
...since the Anschluss.

1593
02:20:17,667 --> 02:20:19,836
- But we have dealt with that.
- I don't...

1594
02:20:20,086 --> 02:20:23,048
The housekeeper told me
that I would find you here.

1595
02:20:23,298 --> 02:20:25,258
The only thing she'd tell me.

1596
02:20:25,508 --> 02:20:28,053
What kind of information
are you looking for?

1597
02:20:28,303 --> 02:20:30,472
When will the captain return?

1598
02:20:30,722 --> 02:20:34,642
Well, he's on his honeymoon trip.
He's not been in touch with us.

1599
02:20:34,893 --> 02:20:39,731
Am I to believe he hasn't communicated
with his children in over a month?

1600
02:20:39,981 --> 02:20:41,900
How many men do you know...

1601
02:20:42,150 --> 02:20:46,071
...who communicate with their children
while honeymooning?

1602
02:20:46,321 --> 02:20:50,075
Upon his return, he'll fill
his proper position in the new order.

1603
02:20:50,325 --> 02:20:52,744
Naturally.
And may I congratulate you...

1604
02:20:52,994 --> 02:20:57,916
...and your people in allowing the
festival to go on tonight as planned.

1605
02:20:58,166 --> 02:21:01,628
Why should it not go on?
Nothing in Austria has changed.

1606
02:21:01,878 --> 02:21:05,215
Singing and music will
show this to the world.

1607
02:21:05,465 --> 02:21:07,801
Austria is the same.

1608
02:21:08,051 --> 02:21:10,970
Heil Hitler.

1609
02:21:11,596 --> 02:21:14,683
Heil Hitler.

1610
02:21:14,849 --> 02:21:16,059
Come, let's go home.

1611
02:21:16,309 --> 02:21:19,771
- Why was he so cross?
- Everybody's cross these days.

1612
02:21:20,021 --> 02:21:23,358
Maybe the flag with the black spider
makes people nervous.

1613
02:21:23,608 --> 02:21:26,528
- Will Father be in trouble?
- He doesn't have to be.

1614
02:21:26,778 --> 02:21:29,489
The thing to do
is to get along with everybody.

1615
02:21:29,739 --> 02:21:31,908
Remember that tonight at the concert.

1616
02:21:32,158 --> 02:21:35,036
Are we really going to sing
before a lot of people?

1617
02:21:35,286 --> 02:21:37,330
Look. The von Trapp Family Singers:

1618
02:21:37,580 --> 02:21:42,127
Liesl, Friedrich, Louisa, Brigitta,
Kurt, Marta and Gretl.

1619
02:21:42,377 --> 02:21:47,215
- Why am I always last?
- Because you are the most important.

1620
02:21:47,465 --> 02:21:50,552
There we go.

1621
02:21:54,973 --> 02:21:58,393
Are you sure Father will approve
of our singing in public?

1622
02:21:58,643 --> 02:22:01,146
He'll be pleased and proud.

1623
02:22:01,396 --> 02:22:03,815
- Liesl, do you think so?
- Don't you trust me?

1624
02:22:04,065 --> 02:22:05,066
No.

1625
02:22:05,316 --> 02:22:07,652
You're a very intelligent girl.

1626
02:22:07,902 --> 02:22:10,780
Liesl. Liesl!

1627
02:22:11,031 --> 02:22:12,991
Rolf!

1628
02:22:13,241 --> 02:22:15,243
I'm so glad to see you.
It's been su...

1629
02:22:15,493 --> 02:22:16,745
Good afternoon.

1630
02:22:16,995 --> 02:22:20,331
Give this to your father
as soon as he's home.

1631
02:22:20,582 --> 02:22:22,417
- He's on his honeymoon.
- I know.

1632
02:22:22,667 --> 02:22:26,421
- You do?
- We make it our business to know all.

1633
02:22:26,671 --> 02:22:29,257
- Who's "we"?
- See that he gets it.

1634
02:22:29,507 --> 02:22:32,427
- What is it?
- It's a telegram from Berlin.

1635
02:22:32,677 --> 02:22:35,388
Don't you want to deliver it yourself?

1636
02:22:35,638 --> 02:22:38,224
I'm occupied
with more important matters.

1637
02:22:38,475 --> 02:22:40,935
And your father had better be too.

1638
02:22:41,186 --> 02:22:44,272
But, Rolf!

1639
02:22:58,495 --> 02:23:02,290
Father! Father!

1640
02:23:05,835 --> 02:23:07,504
Father!

1641
02:23:07,754 --> 02:23:10,423
- We didn't expect you so soon.
- Hello, hello!

1642
02:23:10,674 --> 02:23:13,426
We didn't expect you home
until next week!

1643
02:23:13,677 --> 02:23:19,974
- Did you bring souvenirs from Paris?
- Hello! How are you?

1644
02:23:20,225 --> 02:23:21,476
Why didn't you call us?

1645
02:23:21,726 --> 02:23:23,645
We couldn't get through.

1646
02:23:23,895 --> 02:23:25,855
I had nothing to do with that.

1647
02:23:26,106 --> 02:23:28,233
We came back as fast as we could.

1648
02:23:28,483 --> 02:23:30,652
Well, well, well! We missed you!

1649
02:23:30,902 --> 02:23:33,279
- We missed you!
- We missed kissing you.

1650
02:23:33,530 --> 02:23:36,366
We missed all the noise
in the morning.

1651
02:23:36,616 --> 02:23:40,787
- Mostly, we missed hearing you sing.
- Oh, you came back just in time.

1652
02:23:41,037 --> 02:23:42,747
Look, Fräulein Maria...

1653
02:23:42,997 --> 02:23:44,791
I mean Mother.

1654
02:23:45,041 --> 02:23:47,502
We're going to sing
in the festival tonight.

1655
02:23:47,752 --> 02:23:49,838
We've been having a lovely time!

1656
02:23:50,088 --> 02:23:53,675
We've been rehearsing all...

1657
02:23:53,717 --> 02:23:56,469
Surprise! Surprise!

1658
02:23:56,720 --> 02:24:00,682
Surprises for you on the terrace.

1659
02:24:02,267 --> 02:24:03,601
We'll talk about this.

1660
02:24:03,852 --> 02:24:08,189
I would've told you but you were away.
I had to make a last-minute decision.

1661
02:24:08,440 --> 02:24:10,900
I was fortunate to enter them at all.

1662
02:24:11,151 --> 02:24:15,822
They'll be the talk of the festival.
Imagine, seven children in one family!

1663
02:24:16,072 --> 02:24:18,074
Max!

1664
02:24:18,324 --> 02:24:21,161
Somehow I recall having made it
quite clear to you...

1665
02:24:21,411 --> 02:24:24,122
...how I feel about my family
singing in public!

1666
02:24:24,372 --> 02:24:26,041
The committee was enchanted.

1667
02:24:26,291 --> 02:24:29,461
- What did they say?
- I have never heard such enthusiasm.

1668
02:24:29,711 --> 02:24:34,007
- Don't you think just this once...?
- Absolutely out of the question.

1669
02:24:34,257 --> 02:24:38,511
- Georg, this is for Austria.
- For Austria?

1670
02:24:38,762 --> 02:24:41,973
- There is no Austria!
- But the Anschluss was peaceful.

1671
02:24:42,223 --> 02:24:47,228
- Let's at least be grateful for that.
- Grateful?

1672
02:24:47,604 --> 02:24:49,898
You know, Max...

1673
02:24:50,148 --> 02:24:54,861
...sometimes I don't believe
I know you.

1674
02:24:56,071 --> 02:24:58,031
Father, I forgot.

1675
02:24:58,281 --> 02:25:01,368
This is for you.

1676
02:25:09,668 --> 02:25:13,672
Maria, he has got to at least pretend
to work with these people.

1677
02:25:13,922 --> 02:25:15,423
You must convince him.

1678
02:25:15,674 --> 02:25:18,385
Max, I can't ask him
to be less than he is.

1679
02:25:18,635 --> 02:25:21,971
Then I'll talk to him.
If the children don't sing, well...

1680
02:25:22,222 --> 02:25:25,016
...it will be a reflection
on Austria.

1681
02:25:25,266 --> 02:25:30,146
Oh, I know.
It wouldn't do me any good either.

1682
02:25:33,817 --> 02:25:36,486
Mother? That sounds so nice.

1683
02:25:36,736 --> 02:25:39,447
- I like calling you "Mother".
- I like hearing it.

1684
02:25:39,698 --> 02:25:43,284
You love Father very much.
I can tell you do.

1685
02:25:43,535 --> 02:25:44,411
Very much.

1686
02:25:44,661 --> 02:25:48,415
Mother, what do you do when you
think you love someone?

1687
02:25:48,665 --> 02:25:55,463
I mean, when you stop loving someone
or he stops loving you?

1688
02:25:56,589 --> 02:25:58,842
Well, you cry a little.

1689
02:25:59,092 --> 02:26:01,219
Then you wait for the sun to come out.

1690
02:26:01,469 --> 02:26:02,846
It always does.

1691
02:26:03,096 --> 02:26:06,641
There are so many things I think
I should know but I don't.

1692
02:26:06,891 --> 02:26:08,852
- I really don't.
- How can you?

1693
02:26:09,102 --> 02:26:11,312
Sometimes I feel the world is ending.

1694
02:26:11,563 --> 02:26:14,691
- Then you feel it's just beginning?
- Yes!

1695
02:26:14,941 --> 02:26:19,654
It was that way with me. And for you
it will be just as wonderful.

1696
02:26:19,904 --> 02:26:22,449
Do you really think so?

1697
02:26:22,699 --> 02:26:26,661
{y:i}When you're 16 going on 17

1698
02:26:26,911 --> 02:26:30,915
{y:i}Waiting for life to start

1699
02:26:31,166 --> 02:26:35,003
{y:i}Somebody kind
{y:i}Who touches your mind

1700
02:26:35,253 --> 02:26:39,716
{y:i}Will suddenly touch your heart

1701
02:26:39,966 --> 02:26:41,843
{y:i}When that happens

1702
02:26:42,093 --> 02:26:44,054
{y:i}After it happens

1703
02:26:44,304 --> 02:26:48,141
{y:i}Nothing is quite the same

1704
02:26:48,391 --> 02:26:49,976
{y:i}Somehow I know

1705
02:26:50,226 --> 02:26:52,395
{y:i}I'll jump up and go

1706
02:26:52,645 --> 02:26:56,900
{y:i}If ever he calls my name

1707
02:26:57,150 --> 02:27:01,071
{y:i}Gone are your old ideas of life

1708
02:27:01,321 --> 02:27:05,533
{y:i}The old ideas grow dim

1709
02:27:05,784 --> 02:27:10,872
{y:i}Lo and behold
{y:i}You're someone's wife

1710
02:27:11,122 --> 02:27:17,128
{y:i}And you belong to him

1711
02:27:17,379 --> 02:27:21,675
{y:i}You may think this kind of adventure

1712
02:27:21,925 --> 02:27:26,596
{y:i}Never may come to you

1713
02:27:26,846 --> 02:27:32,852
{y:i}Darling, 16 going on 17

1714
02:27:33,103 --> 02:27:37,774
{y:i}Wait a year...

1715
02:27:38,024 --> 02:27:43,405
{y:i}- I'll wait a year
{y:i}...or two

1716
02:27:43,655 --> 02:27:51,663
{y:i}Just wait a year...

1717
02:27:51,913 --> 02:28:02,048
{y:i}... or two

1718
02:28:02,966 --> 02:28:06,052
Liesl.

1719
02:28:20,984 --> 02:28:23,028
What is it?

1720
02:28:23,278 --> 02:28:24,863
Berlin.

1721
02:28:25,113 --> 02:28:26,990
They've offered me a commission.

1722
02:28:27,240 --> 02:28:29,784
I've been requested
to accept immediately...

1723
02:28:30,035 --> 02:28:34,372
...and report to their naval base
at Bremerhaven tomorrow.

1724
02:28:34,622 --> 02:28:38,585
I knew this would happen.
I didn't think it would be so soon.

1725
02:28:38,835 --> 02:28:43,340
To refuse them would be fatal
for all of us.

1726
02:28:43,590 --> 02:28:48,887
And joining them would be unthinkable.

1727
02:28:53,516 --> 02:28:55,352
Get the children all together.

1728
02:28:55,602 --> 02:28:59,522
Don't say anything to worry them.
Just get them ready.

1729
02:28:59,773 --> 02:29:03,693
We've got to get out of Austria...

1730
02:29:03,818 --> 02:29:07,072
...and this house...

1731
02:29:07,864 --> 02:29:10,784
...tonight.

1732
02:29:53,201 --> 02:29:55,620
This strains my back
and breaks my heart...

1733
02:29:55,870 --> 02:29:58,707
...when I think of the children
missing the festival.

1734
02:29:58,957 --> 02:30:01,668
By your announcement
we'll be over the border.

1735
02:30:01,918 --> 02:30:04,295
Do you appreciate
the sacrifice I'm making?

1736
02:30:04,546 --> 02:30:05,839
You have no choice.

1737
02:30:06,089 --> 02:30:09,342
I know. That's why I'm making it.

1738
02:30:09,592 --> 02:30:11,720
Why doesn't Father turn the motor on?

1739
02:30:11,970 --> 02:30:16,891
Because he doesn't want anyone
to hear us.

1740
02:30:18,977 --> 02:30:21,479
What will Frau Schmidt and Franz say?

1741
02:30:21,730 --> 02:30:25,233
They'll be able to answer honestly
they didn't know anything.

1742
02:30:25,483 --> 02:30:29,696
- Will we be coming back here?
- Someday, Liesl. I do hope someday.

1743
02:30:29,946 --> 02:30:36,369
Are Father and Uncle Max going to push
the car all the way to Switzerland?

1744
02:31:01,644 --> 02:31:04,147
Something wrong
with your car, captain?

1745
02:31:04,397 --> 02:31:08,193
Yes, we couldn't get it started.

1746
02:31:10,445 --> 02:31:13,198
Karl.

1747
02:31:13,656 --> 02:31:18,620
Fix Captain von Trapp's car
so that it will start.

1748
02:31:40,308 --> 02:31:43,645
Excellent, Karl.

1749
02:31:45,522 --> 02:31:48,942
I've not asked you where you
and your family are going.

1750
02:31:49,192 --> 02:31:51,277
Nor have you asked me why I'm here.

1751
02:31:51,528 --> 02:31:55,907
Apparently we both suffer
from a deplorable lack of curiosity.

1752
02:31:56,157 --> 02:31:58,618
You never answered the telegram...

1753
02:31:58,868 --> 02:32:02,163
...from the Admiral of the Navy
of the Third Reich.

1754
02:32:02,414 --> 02:32:04,833
I was under the impression,
Herr Zeller...

1755
02:32:05,083 --> 02:32:09,087
...that the contents of telegrams
in Austria are private!

1756
02:32:09,337 --> 02:32:13,758
At least, the Austria I know.

1757
02:32:14,009 --> 02:32:15,635
I have my orders...

1758
02:32:15,885 --> 02:32:18,054
...to take you
to Bremerhaven tonight...

1759
02:32:18,304 --> 02:32:21,391
...where you will accept
your commission.

1760
02:32:21,641 --> 02:32:27,439
I'm afraid that's going to be
quite impossible.

1761
02:32:27,689 --> 02:32:29,733
You see, we...

1762
02:32:29,983 --> 02:32:33,653
...all of us, the entire family,
will be...

1763
02:32:33,903 --> 02:32:35,989
...singing in the festival tonight.

1764
02:32:36,239 --> 02:32:39,075
As a matter of fact, we're going now.

1765
02:32:39,325 --> 02:32:42,162
We couldn't possibly
let them down now.

1766
02:32:42,412 --> 02:32:45,373
- I just hope we're not too late.
- Yes.

1767
02:32:45,623 --> 02:32:48,501
You ask me to believe that you,
Captain von Trapp...

1768
02:32:48,752 --> 02:32:50,378
...are singing in a concert?

1769
02:32:50,628 --> 02:32:54,841
Believe me, it will be a performance
beyond anything even I've dreamt of.

1770
02:32:55,091 --> 02:33:00,180
Like you, Herr Zeller,
I, too, am a man of hidden talents.

1771
02:33:00,430 --> 02:33:01,806
Yes.

1772
02:33:02,057 --> 02:33:06,019
Here, program.

1773
02:33:09,564 --> 02:33:11,858
It says only the names
of the children.

1774
02:33:12,108 --> 02:33:15,403
It says
the von Trapp Family Singers...

1775
02:33:15,653 --> 02:33:20,200
...and I am the head of
the von Trapp family, am I not?

1776
02:33:20,992 --> 02:33:25,372
And these travel clothes
that you're all wearing?

1777
02:33:25,622 --> 02:33:27,165
Our costumes, naturally.

1778
02:33:27,415 --> 02:33:32,295
This night air is not good
for the children's voices.

1779
02:33:37,050 --> 02:33:41,554
Well, a slight delay in my orders
will not be serious.

1780
02:33:41,805 --> 02:33:43,932
Therefore...

1781
02:33:44,182 --> 02:33:46,476
...you will sing.

1782
02:33:46,726 --> 02:33:48,853
You will all sing.

1783
02:33:49,104 --> 02:33:52,273
But only because that's what I want.

1784
02:33:52,524 --> 02:33:56,486
It will demonstrate that nothing
in Austria has changed.

1785
02:33:56,736 --> 02:33:58,988
And when you have finished singing...

1786
02:33:59,239 --> 02:34:03,451
...you, Captain von Trapp, will be
taken to Bremerhaven.

1787
02:34:03,702 --> 02:34:06,413
Now, if you will all get
into your car...

1788
02:34:06,663 --> 02:34:11,084
...we will escort the von Trapp Family
Singers to the festival.

1789
02:34:11,334 --> 02:34:13,545
No escort will be necessary.

1790
02:34:13,795 --> 02:34:16,923
Necessary? A pleasure, captain.

1791
02:34:17,173 --> 02:34:22,012
After all, we would not want you
to get lost in the crowds.

1792
02:34:22,262 --> 02:34:24,180
Would we?

1793
02:34:24,431 --> 02:34:27,100
No.

1794
02:34:41,740 --> 02:34:44,951
{y:i}Sew, a needle pulling thread

1795
02:34:45,201 --> 02:34:48,413
{y:i}La, a note to follow sew

1796
02:34:48,663 --> 02:34:52,083
{y:i}Tea, a drink with jam and bread

1797
02:34:52,334 --> 02:34:56,129
{y:i}A drink with jam and bread

1798
02:34:56,379 --> 02:34:59,132
{y:i}Tea with jam, jam and bread

1799
02:34:59,382 --> 02:35:01,217
{y:i}Tea with jam and bread

1800
02:35:01,468 --> 02:35:05,472
{y:i}Jam and bread
{y:i}Tea with jam, jam and bread

1801
02:35:05,722 --> 02:35:09,351
{y:i}Tea with jam
{y:i}Jam and bread

1802
02:35:13,772 --> 02:35:17,067
{y:i}With jam
{y:i}A, B, C

1803
02:35:17,317 --> 02:35:20,528
{y:i}With jam
{y:i}Do-re-mi

1804
02:35:20,779 --> 02:35:24,366
{y:i}Tea with jam and bread

1805
02:35:24,616 --> 02:35:25,950
{y:i}With jam and bread

1806
02:35:26,201 --> 02:35:27,702
{y:i}With jam
{y:i}With jam...

1807
02:35:27,952 --> 02:35:36,795
{y:i}... and bread

1808
02:35:58,692 --> 02:36:02,946
My fellow Austrians...

1809
02:36:03,196 --> 02:36:08,868
...I shall not be seeing you again,
perhaps for a very long time.

1810
02:36:09,119 --> 02:36:12,122
I would like to sing for you now...

1811
02:36:12,372 --> 02:36:15,458
...a love song.

1812
02:36:15,917 --> 02:36:19,170
I know you share this love.

1813
02:36:19,421 --> 02:36:24,634
I pray that you will never let it die.

1814
02:36:35,895 --> 02:36:39,357
{y:i}Edelweiss

1815
02:36:39,607 --> 02:36:42,861
{y:i}Edelweiss

1816
02:36:43,111 --> 02:36:48,992
{y:i}Every morning you greet me

1817
02:36:49,242 --> 02:36:52,328
{y:i}Small and white

1818
02:36:52,579 --> 02:36:55,665
{y:i}Clean and bright

1819
02:36:55,915 --> 02:37:01,546
{y:i}You look happy to meet me

1820
02:37:01,796 --> 02:37:03,840
{y:i}Blossom of snow

1821
02:37:04,090 --> 02:37:07,677
{y:i}May you bloom and grow

1822
02:37:07,927 --> 02:37:14,809
{y:i}Bloom and grow forever

1823
02:37:15,060 --> 02:37:17,604
{y:i}Edelweiss

1824
02:37:17,854 --> 02:37:20,440
{y:i}Edelweiss

1825
02:37:20,690 --> 02:37:28,239
{y:i}Bless my homeland forever

1826
02:37:31,201 --> 02:37:34,287
{y:i}Edelweiss

1827
02:37:34,537 --> 02:37:37,374
{y:i}Edel...

1828
02:37:43,254 --> 02:37:46,299
{y:i}Small and white

1829
02:37:46,549 --> 02:37:49,386
{y:i}Clean and bright

1830
02:37:49,636 --> 02:37:55,558
{y:i}You look happy to meet me

1831
02:37:55,809 --> 02:37:57,727
{y:i}Blossom of snow

1832
02:37:57,977 --> 02:38:01,815
{y:i}May you bloom and grow

1833
02:38:02,065 --> 02:38:08,279
{y:i}Bloom and grow forever

1834
02:38:08,530 --> 02:38:11,491
{y:i}Edelweiss

1835
02:38:11,741 --> 02:38:14,869
{y:i}Edelweiss

1836
02:38:15,120 --> 02:38:24,838
{y:i}Bless my homeland forever

1837
02:38:41,062 --> 02:38:43,106
I think it'll work.

1838
02:38:43,356 --> 02:38:45,358
I shall miss all of you.

1839
02:38:45,608 --> 02:38:49,571
I shall miss the money
I could have made with you.

1840
02:38:51,489 --> 02:38:53,533
Thank you, ladies and gentlemen.

1841
02:38:53,783 --> 02:38:56,453
The competition has come
to its conclusion...

1842
02:38:56,703 --> 02:39:00,540
...except, we don't know
yet what that conclusion will be.

1843
02:39:00,790 --> 02:39:03,335
And while the judges
arrive at their decision...

1844
02:39:03,585 --> 02:39:06,629
...I have been given permission
to offer you an encore.

1845
02:39:06,880 --> 02:39:10,091
This will be the last opportunity
the von Trapps will have...

1846
02:39:10,342 --> 02:39:13,553
...of singing together
for a long, long time.

1847
02:39:13,803 --> 02:39:17,807
Even now, officials are waiting
in this auditorium...

1848
02:39:18,058 --> 02:39:20,727
...to escort Captain von Trapp
to his new command...

1849
02:39:20,977 --> 02:39:24,522
...in the naval forces
of the Third Reich.

1850
02:39:29,235 --> 02:39:34,157
And so, ladies and gentlemen,
the family von Trapp again...

1851
02:39:34,407 --> 02:39:38,203
...to bid you farewell.

1852
02:39:43,917 --> 02:39:47,545
{y:i}There's a sad sort of clanging
{y:i}From the clock in the hall

1853
02:39:47,796 --> 02:39:51,383
{y:i}And the bells in the steeple too

1854
02:39:51,633 --> 02:39:55,470
{y:i}And up in the nursery
{y:i}An absurd little bird

1855
02:39:55,720 --> 02:39:58,098
{y:i}Is popping out to say "coo-coo"

1856
02:39:58,348 --> 02:40:00,016
{y:i}Coo-coo

1857
02:40:00,266 --> 02:40:01,267
{y:i}Coo-coo

1858
02:40:01,518 --> 02:40:03,103
{y:i}Regretfully they tell us

1859
02:40:03,353 --> 02:40:05,146
{y:i}But firmly they compel us

1860
02:40:05,397 --> 02:40:08,108
{y:i}To say goodbye

1861
02:40:08,358 --> 02:40:15,031
{y:i}To you

1862
02:40:16,449 --> 02:40:20,036
{y:i}So long, farewell
{y:i}Auf Wiedersehen, good night

1863
02:40:20,286 --> 02:40:25,542
{y:i}We hate to go
{y:i}And miss this pretty sight

1864
02:40:30,213 --> 02:40:33,758
{y:i}So long, farewell
{y:i}Auf Wiedersehen, adieu

1865
02:40:34,009 --> 02:40:39,180
{y:i}Adieu, adieu
{y:i}To you and you and you

1866
02:40:43,977 --> 02:40:47,522
{y:i}So long, farewell
{y:i}Auf Wiedersehen, goodbye

1867
02:40:47,772 --> 02:40:49,399
{y:i}We flit, we float

1868
02:40:49,649 --> 02:40:52,902
{y:i}We fleetly flee, we fly

1869
02:40:57,741 --> 02:41:01,494
{y:i}So long, farewell
{y:i}Auf Wiedersehen, goodbye

1870
02:41:01,745 --> 02:41:05,123
{y:i}The sun has gone to bed
{y:i}And so must I

1871
02:41:05,373 --> 02:41:09,169
{y:i}Goodbye

1872
02:41:09,419 --> 02:41:15,091
{y:i}Goodbye

1873
02:41:15,342 --> 02:41:20,555
{y:i}Goodbye

1874
02:41:20,805 --> 02:41:29,481
{y:i}Goodbye

1875
02:41:59,719 --> 02:42:03,848
Ladies and gentlemen, I have here the
decision of our distinguished judges.

1876
02:42:04,099 --> 02:42:07,060
We will start with the award
for third prize.

1877
02:42:07,310 --> 02:42:09,646
For this honor,
the judges have named...

1878
02:42:09,896 --> 02:42:14,526
...the first soloist of the choir
of St. Agatha's Church in Murback.

1879
02:42:14,776 --> 02:42:18,530
Fräulein Schweiger.

1880
02:42:53,898 --> 02:42:59,362
Second prize
to The Toby Reiser Quintet.

1881
02:43:25,847 --> 02:43:30,018
And the first prize,
the highest honor in all Austria...

1882
02:43:30,268 --> 02:43:34,898
...the von Trapp Family Singers.

1883
02:43:45,700 --> 02:43:48,953
The family von Trapp.

1884
02:44:02,008 --> 02:44:04,594
They're gone!

1885
02:44:21,778 --> 02:44:23,154
Come with me.

1886
02:44:27,033 --> 02:44:29,703
Quickly, quickly.

1887
02:44:29,953 --> 02:44:32,622
I have a place you can hide.

1888
02:44:32,872 --> 02:44:37,669
Slowly, slowly.

1889
02:44:47,137 --> 02:44:49,723
Open this gate.

1890
02:44:49,973 --> 02:44:53,059
Good evening.

1891
02:44:54,060 --> 02:44:57,063
Hurry up, woman.

1892
02:45:15,373 --> 02:45:16,541
Two men in there.

1893
02:45:16,791 --> 02:45:18,626
Six of you cover the yard.

1894
02:45:18,877 --> 02:45:22,672
You two, cover the corridor.

1895
02:45:35,643 --> 02:45:39,230
Reverend Mother, we didn't realize
we put the abbey in this danger.

1896
02:45:39,481 --> 02:45:41,983
No, Maria, it was right for you
to come here.

1897
02:45:42,233 --> 02:45:44,069
We thought we might borrow
your car.

1898
02:45:44,319 --> 02:45:46,571
I'm afraid our car will do you
no good now.

1899
02:45:46,821 --> 02:45:48,448
I've been listening to the wires.

1900
02:45:48,698 --> 02:45:52,702
The borders have just been closed.

1901
02:45:57,082 --> 02:46:00,377
All right,
if the borders are closed...

1902
02:46:00,627 --> 02:46:04,297
...then we'll drive up into the hills
and go over the mountains on foot.

1903
02:46:04,547 --> 02:46:06,549
- The children...
- We'll help them.

1904
02:46:06,800 --> 02:46:09,803
We can do it without help, Father.

1905
02:46:10,053 --> 02:46:11,846
Maria.

1906
02:46:12,097 --> 02:46:14,724
You will not be alone.
Remember:

1907
02:46:14,974 --> 02:46:18,812
"I will lift up mine eyes unto
the hills from whence cometh my help".

1908
02:46:19,062 --> 02:46:21,272
Yes, Mother.

1909
02:46:21,523 --> 02:46:25,193
- I'm scared.
- Me too.

1910
02:46:35,787 --> 02:46:38,456
God be with you.

1911
02:46:43,878 --> 02:46:45,422
- Mother.
- Yes?

1912
02:46:45,672 --> 02:46:48,883
Would it help if we sang
about our favorite things?

1913
02:46:49,134 --> 02:46:52,137
No, darling.
This is one time it would not help.

1914
02:46:52,387 --> 02:46:57,100
You must be very quiet.
Hold tight to me.

1915
02:49:10,233 --> 02:49:12,944
Let's try the roof.

1916
02:50:07,707 --> 02:50:11,503
Rolf, please.

1917
02:50:15,048 --> 02:50:18,134
No, wait.

1918
02:50:26,351 --> 02:50:28,561
Maria.

1919
02:50:28,812 --> 02:50:31,731
Children.

1920
02:50:34,275 --> 02:50:37,445
It's you we want, not them.

1921
02:50:37,696 --> 02:50:40,240
Put that down.

1922
02:50:40,490 --> 02:50:45,286
Not another move,
or I'll shoot.

1923
02:50:45,495 --> 02:50:49,040
You're only a boy.

1924
02:50:50,542 --> 02:50:55,714
- You don't really belong to them.
- Stay where you are.

1925
02:50:55,964 --> 02:50:58,967
Come away with us.

1926
02:50:59,217 --> 02:51:00,719
Before it's too late.

1927
02:51:00,969 --> 02:51:04,806
Not another step.
I'll kill you.

1928
02:51:05,598 --> 02:51:09,686
- You give that to me, Rolf.
- Did you hear me?

1929
02:51:09,936 --> 02:51:13,023
I'll kill you.

1930
02:51:13,398 --> 02:51:16,401
Rolf.

1931
02:51:33,209 --> 02:51:36,129
You'll never be one of them.

1932
02:51:37,339 --> 02:51:39,049
Lieutenant!

1933
02:51:39,299 --> 02:51:40,383
They're here!

1934
02:51:40,633 --> 02:51:42,135
They're here, lieutenant!

1935
02:52:23,510 --> 02:52:26,221
Reverend Mother.

1936
02:52:26,471 --> 02:52:29,224
I have sinned.

1937
02:52:29,474 --> 02:52:33,103
I too, Reverend Mother.

1938
02:52:33,269 --> 02:52:37,065
What is this sin, my children?

1939
02:53:55,852 --> 02:53:56,811
Subtitles by
Gelula/SDI
